WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.134
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4563.

2
00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.268
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:02.646
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:02.646 --> 00:00:05.291
Okay, so today is Thursday February twenty-fifth,

5
00:00:05.291 --> 00:00:14.740
2016 and I'm here again for our second interview with Mr. Morris Prater in his home in Iowa . . . I always think I'm saying it wrong,

6
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.008
but I think it's Iowa, Louisiana.

7
00:00:17.008 --> 00:00:26.457
And for the first interview we kind of talked about your family history, and parents' names, and grandparents' names, and origins, and stuff like that.

8
00:00:26.457 --> 00:00:30.614
So today we kind of want to pick up there again,

9
00:00:30.614 --> 00:00:39.307
and kind of the first question I wanted to ask you to kind of get this started was about your neighborhood growing up.

10
00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:48.000
Could you just describe your home, what it looked like, where it was, and like your neighbor’s homes and things like that?

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.593
<v MORRIS PRATER>Okay.

12
00:00:48.593 --> 00:00:53.340
Yes I remember it was a large house.

13
00:00:53.340 --> 00:01:00.459
I don't know who they rented it from, but it was rented.

14
00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:11.732
Mom and Dad was renting it, and it was a large house, but we didn't use all the house.

15
00:01:11.732 --> 00:01:15.292
We just used some of it.

16
00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:20.038
Now the neighbors was all a distance apart.

17
00:01:20.038 --> 00:01:22.411
Wasn't nobody close by.

18
00:01:22.411 --> 00:01:31.904
It was dirt roads, and just wagon trails going from one house to the other one.

19
00:01:31.904 --> 00:01:39.024
And us kids would get together and play, meet up and play.

20
00:01:39.024 --> 00:01:49.703
A lot of times they come into the house, and we'd all play and have a good time.

21
00:01:49.703 --> 00:01:56.230
But all the neighbors wasn't like it is now, close by.

22
00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:03.943
They was all within a quarter of a mile or half a mile.

23
00:02:03.943 --> 00:02:11.062
Wasn't too many houses in the neighborhood when I was growing up.

24
00:02:11.062 --> 00:02:15.215
A few of my uncles and aunts.

25
00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:16.995
Had the Braxtons.

26
00:02:16.995 --> 00:02:22.335
They was all up Evergreen [Road], up that way.

27
00:02:22.335 --> 00:02:22.928
They

28
00:02:22.928 --> 00:02:26.488
<c.p>called that . . . That was the nickname.

29
00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:32.421
I don't know where they got the name from, Saprack.

30
00:02:32.421 --> 00:02:39.541
It was . . . That's where my grandfather stayed up there, my mom's dad.

31
00:02:39.541 --> 00:02:52.000
That was approximately a mile and a half closer to my house than where we were staying where grandfather was.

32
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.635
So it . . . And the neighbors was . . . My aunt and uncles was around there about.

33
00:02:58.635 --> 00:03:01.953
It wasn't close like it is now.

34
00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:04.323
This house is over here.

35
00:03:04.323 --> 00:03:08.115
Maybe the other house is over that way.

36
00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:10.958
Another one way over that way.

37
00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:15.698
It just had wagon trails . . . wagon trail to each house.

38
00:03:15.698 --> 00:03:24.703
We'd leave from our house, well if we’d had to go to my auntie’s we'd go to the right.

39
00:03:24.703 --> 00:03:36.552
And we had to go up grandfather's that was we just going up north like you see, up that way, but it's all wagon trails.

40
00:03:36.552 --> 00:03:48.401
This one take here say, "Well we going to so-and-so's house you going to turn up there," or if you’re going to somebody else’s house.

41
00:03:48.401 --> 00:03:56.932
And we stayed there until my . . . Well my aunt and uncle moved in that big house with us.

42
00:03:56.932 --> 00:03:59.302
That's Butch mom and dad.

43
00:03:59.302 --> 00:04:01.198
He wasn't born then.

44
00:04:01.198 --> 00:04:02.620
No, Butch wasn't.

45
00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:09.255
And so they moved in the house with us and stayed for a while,

46
00:04:09.255 --> 00:04:18.260
and then they bought a house on Old Spanish Trail what was just torn down a few weeks ago.

47
00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.000
That's where Butch and them was born over there.

48
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:31.402
And I used to go up to my grandfather's and stay up there a lot.

49
00:04:31.402 --> 00:04:40.924
I liked to stay up there because my uncle . . . I had two uncles and they was teenagers.

50
00:04:40.924 --> 00:04:47.085
So I liked to go up there and be with them.

51
00:04:47.085 --> 00:04:52.127
So they'd roam the woods and all that stuff.

52
00:04:52.127 --> 00:04:59.408
It was . . . I had a good life, fun life and all like that.

53
00:04:59.408 --> 00:05:07.250
But after we . . . Neighbors . . . We moved, then we didn't have any neighbors right close.

54
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:14.532
That was all just a distance apart when we moved on Prater Road.

55
00:05:14.532 --> 00:05:19.573
We moved there and we didn't have any lights.

56
00:05:19.573 --> 00:05:20.693
Cut wood.

57
00:05:20.693 --> 00:05:24.054
Had a wood stove, wood heater.

58
00:05:24.054 --> 00:05:29.095
That's what practically everybody had out in Mossville [Louisiana].

59
00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:32.456
Didn't have electricity out that way.

60
00:05:32.456 --> 00:05:38.617
So it was . . . it's neighborly and a good place to be.

61
00:05:38.617 --> 00:05:41.978
It's quiet and everything back then.

62
00:05:41.978 --> 00:05:46.459
So it was a good life, good life.

63
00:05:46.459 --> 00:05:55.421
<c.p>But after we moved on Prater Road, that's where things kind of changed a little bit.

64
00:05:55.421 --> 00:06:05.503
Prater Road was there, but it wasn't paved, and it didn't have any [shell?] or anything on it.

65
00:06:05.503 --> 00:06:09.424
It was just a plain dirt road.

66
00:06:09.424 --> 00:06:14.465
So that's where I started doing more house work.

67
00:06:14.465 --> 00:06:26.228
So that's where I say I lost all of my childhood days back then because my mom was sick a lot,

68
00:06:26.228 --> 00:06:30.149
and I missed a lot of school.

69
00:06:30.149 --> 00:06:34.630
I didn't get a chance to finish school.

70
00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:43.032
Missed a lot of school on account of she was sick and we had babies.

71
00:06:43.032 --> 00:06:55.354
I had to just take care of them and start doing the house cleaning or cooking and washing and stuff like that.

72
00:06:55.354 --> 00:07:03.756
All of us kids is anywhere from year and a half to two years old,

73
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:11.598
so it always was mostly a youngster there had to be taken care of.

74
00:07:11.598 --> 00:07:14.399
I was the oldest one.

75
00:07:14.399 --> 00:07:20.000
Had a brother under me then I had sister.

76
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.816
So I got on Prater Road,

77
00:07:23.816 --> 00:07:37.172
and after got up to that age then my sister she was getting a little older because she's the third child.

78
00:07:37.172 --> 00:07:49.892
So that's my time then I started leaving the house going to work at odd jobs and stuff like that.

79
00:07:49.892 --> 00:07:54.344
Then she started taking care of mom.

80
00:07:54.344 --> 00:08:04.519
And that's when I lost my childhood times during that time because it just left me.

81
00:08:04.519 --> 00:08:11.515
But other than that I had a good life in Mossville.

82
00:08:11.515 --> 00:08:19.783
I was born in Mossville, and I was born and raised right there.

83
00:08:19.783 --> 00:08:26.143
Stayed right there for eighty-five years right there in Mossville.

84
00:08:26.143 --> 00:08:29.959
So I had a good life.

85
00:08:29.959 --> 00:08:31.231
Can't complain.

86
00:08:31.231 --> 00:08:40.135
But as I said . . . getting back to my grandfather that he was a farmer.

87
00:08:40.135 --> 00:08:42.043
Not a farmer.

88
00:08:42.043 --> 00:08:47.131
He's what they called [truck patched like that?].

89
00:08:47.131 --> 00:08:52.855
So he had vegetables, stuff like that, hog, cows.

90
00:08:52.855 --> 00:09:01.759
So it was . . . I liked to be up there because my uncles and all,

91
00:09:01.759 --> 00:09:10.027
they was teenage boys, and we roamed around like that in the woods.

92
00:09:10.027 --> 00:09:17.658
I'd go home on the weekends and be at home like that.

93
00:09:17.658 --> 00:09:32.286
So and when I left then started running around and after I started to work started running around at night a little bit.

94
00:09:32.286 --> 00:09:38.010
Then I got older I went in the service.

95
00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:45.642
Then after come back out of the service that was it then.

96
00:09:45.642 --> 00:09:52.638
I wasn't around home too much, but I'm still in Mossville.

97
00:09:52.638 --> 00:09:58.998
<c.p>And we moved . . . after I got married we moved on.

98
00:09:58.998 --> 00:10:05.358
Bought a place there and moved there, right in Mossville.

99
00:10:05.358 --> 00:10:14.262
Me and my wife, we all moved there, and we built a house there.

100
00:10:14.262 --> 00:10:16.806
Kids was born there.

101
00:10:16.806 --> 00:10:18.078
Two girls.

102
00:10:18.078 --> 00:10:32.070
Well my oldest one, she wasn't born there, but the other two was born right at the house we just left from.

103
00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:37.157
When we moved there where we moved from,

104
00:10:37.157 --> 00:10:53.057
we moved there it only had three . . . two families in that . . . That was where we stay and call that little place there Green Briar Subdivision.

105
00:10:53.057 --> 00:10:54.329
Green Briar.

106
00:10:54.329 --> 00:11:06.413
And it was only two houses in there when me and the wife moved in and built our house.

107
00:11:06.413 --> 00:11:10.865
It was the . . . What was his name?

108
00:11:10.865 --> 00:11:11.501
Harris.

109
00:11:11.501 --> 00:11:17.225
Harris was staying there and [Kellis?] stayed in there.

110
00:11:17.225 --> 00:11:22.313
That was the only two houses in there.

111
00:11:22.313 --> 00:11:34.397
But now we just moved out now and I don't know exactly how many houses is in there now.

112
00:11:34.397 --> 00:11:43.301
It's probably twenty or twenty-five, thirty houses in Green Briar neighborhood we stayed in.

113
00:11:43.301 --> 00:11:50.933
And the houses, like I say, the houses were so far apart.

114
00:11:50.933 --> 00:11:56.020
They had them all scattered around and about.

115
00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.200
Then they started building roads.

116
00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:04.924
They built Evergreen in I don't know what year.

117
00:12:04.924 --> 00:12:15.100
I don't remember what year they . . . Prater Road was, but it was probably in the '40s.

118
00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:21.460
I done forgot what year we moved on Prater Road.

119
00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.640
I can't remember what year.

120
00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:31.000
But we were the first house on Prater Road.

121
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:42.652
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were . . . You took care of the family when your mom got sick when you were . . . How old were you?

122
00:12:42.652 --> 00:12:44.872
<v PRATER>Oh, I don't know.

123
00:12:44.872 --> 00:12:49.866
I would say about thirteen, fourteen . . . in that area.

124
00:12:49.866 --> 00:12:53.195
Maybe a little earlier than that.

125
00:12:53.195 --> 00:12:54.860
I can't remember.

126
00:12:54.860 --> 00:13:02.073
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did you miss out on having to be responsible?

127
00:13:02.073 --> 00:13:04.848
<v PRATER>Well I missed my schooling.

128
00:13:04.848 --> 00:13:17.610
I missed lots of schooling because I would be out of school any . . . sometimes it'd be two or three weeks at a time.

129
00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:22.049
And so I would just miss so much.

130
00:13:22.049 --> 00:13:34.256
Like I said, it was eleven of us kids and it was we all anywhere from a year or two years apart.

131
00:13:34.256 --> 00:13:37.585
So it was always a youngster.

132
00:13:37.585 --> 00:13:49.793
Mostly I'd be years there and I was the oldest so that was my job to start doing the house work, cooking.

133
00:13:49.793 --> 00:13:52.567
I learned how to cook.

134
00:13:52.567 --> 00:14:02.000
I learned how to cook, and it was always clothes to wash, especially baby clothes. [laughs]

135
00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:02.853
<v ARSENEAULT>Never ending.

136
00:14:02.853 --> 00:14:03.706
<v PRATER>Yes, right.

137
00:14:03.706 --> 00:14:06.265
It wasn't like it is now.

138
00:14:06.265 --> 00:14:10.529
They got Pampers and stuff but this was cloth diapers.

139
00:14:10.529 --> 00:14:14.368
It's just so you had to keep them up.

140
00:14:14.368 --> 00:14:15.647
And cut wood.

141
00:14:15.647 --> 00:14:19.485
Now we had a wood stove and all that.

142
00:14:19.485 --> 00:14:21.618
We didn't have any electricity.

143
00:14:21.618 --> 00:14:24.176
<v ARSENEAULT>What's this . . . So a wood stove.

144
00:14:24.176 --> 00:14:26.309
So you cut the wood?

145
00:14:26.309 --> 00:14:26.735
<v PRATER>Yes.

146
00:14:26.735 --> 00:14:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And what . . . How do you . . . How does that work?

147
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.284
<v PRATER>Well we had a crosscut saw, one of these long ones.

148
00:14:36.284 --> 00:14:42.529
So long anyone get on this end and get on the other end.

149
00:14:42.529 --> 00:14:50.216
And me and my brother, he was just about a year and a half, two years,

150
00:14:50.216 --> 00:14:58.382
younger than me, so we'd have to cut the wood and slab it up, bust it up.

151
00:14:58.382 --> 00:15:01.265
And we had a wood stove.

152
00:15:01.265 --> 00:15:03.667
That's what we cooked on.

153
00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:06.549
That was our job to do.

154
00:15:06.549 --> 00:15:07.510
Keep that.

155
00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:08.471
Do that.

156
00:15:08.471 --> 00:15:09.431
Because my

157
00:15:09.431 --> 00:15:15.676
<c.p>dad, he was working in Westlake [Louisiana] at the saw mill, like that.

158
00:15:15.676 --> 00:15:27.206
One time they was working on the road though, and I think I told you that the last time about the WPA or something.

159
00:15:27.206 --> 00:15:32.971
That's where the government job like that was work during the Depression.

160
00:15:32.971 --> 00:15:40.657
So it's . . . We had clothes and stuff to eat but we didn't think anything about it.

161
00:15:40.657 --> 00:15:50.745
It ain't like now they got different things to eat and they won't . . . They don't want this and don't want that.

162
00:15:50.745 --> 00:15:53.627
But we ate whatever was cooked.

163
00:15:53.627 --> 00:15:57.471
We ate it and didn't worry about it.

164
00:15:57.471 --> 00:15:59.392
Didn't think about it.

165
00:15:59.392 --> 00:16:00.833
That was it.

166
00:16:00.833 --> 00:16:02.275
That was it.

167
00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you ever remember waiting in line for rations or anything like that?

168
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:18.540
<v PRATER>Well yes, my . . . Like I said, back during the Depression it wasn't any money for the work being done.

169
00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:22.054
That work was government work like that.

170
00:16:22.054 --> 00:16:32.598
They had a place set up at the store, and so you could there and buy at the store like that.

171
00:16:32.598 --> 00:16:35.611
But you didn't have any money.

172
00:16:35.611 --> 00:16:38.121
You didn't see the money.

173
00:16:38.121 --> 00:16:41.636
You just go buy what you needed.

174
00:16:41.636 --> 00:16:44.146
So and then after that,

175
00:16:44.146 --> 00:16:56.197
my dad left that and found this sawmill job where they started getting a little money then because times start changing then, you see.

176
00:16:56.197 --> 00:16:58.707
But it's lots different now.

177
00:16:58.707 --> 00:17:08.247
Back then because when I was coming up like that then blacks only had certain place they could go.

178
00:17:08.247 --> 00:17:12.264
Couldn't go sit down like you can now,

179
00:17:12.264 --> 00:17:26.322
because . . . Well it didn't bother me because I was brought up during that time so I know how far I could go and this that and the other.

180
00:17:26.322 --> 00:17:30.339
So it didn't really bother me, you see.

181
00:17:30.339 --> 00:17:34.356
So that made a whole lot of difference.

182
00:17:34.356 --> 00:17:36.364
But it's changed now.

183
00:17:36.364 --> 00:17:42.891
It's . . . You can go anywhere you want to eat and nobody bother you.

184
00:17:42.891 --> 00:17:46.908
So it makes a whole lot of difference.

185
00:17:46.908 --> 00:18:00.967
I try to tell them all the time it's lot different than what it were back then because they have a right to do whatever they can do.

186
00:18:00.967 --> 00:18:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any challenges after serving in the service to come back and see?

187
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:20.597
<v PRATER>Well yes, because when I went in the service . . . Well I went in ‘51 and it was just start changing in the early '50s.

188
00:18:20.597 --> 00:18:26.878
So when I went in and I went . . . I was stationed in Virginia.

189
00:18:26.878 --> 00:18:35.092
When I left, because you had certain place you had to get on to ride the bus.

190
00:18:35.092 --> 00:18:39.924
You had to go to the back and all that.

191
00:18:39.924 --> 00:18:44.273
So I got there, well it had start changing.

192
00:18:44.273 --> 00:18:49.105
I can ride the bus sit down anywhere I want.

193
00:18:49.105 --> 00:18:53.454
Ride anywhere or go anywhere I wanted to go.

194
00:18:53.454 --> 00:18:59.252
And it made it different too when I was in the service.

195
00:18:59.252 --> 00:19:04.567
You had little more rights when you were in the service.

196
00:19:04.567 --> 00:19:07.950
But anyway, it made a change then.

197
00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:13.748
And when I come back in '53, well it was . . . some better.

198
00:19:13.748 --> 00:19:18.097
But it . . . Like I tell them all the time,

199
00:19:18.097 --> 00:19:27.761
it was different when I was coming up because certain places you could go and certain places you couldn't go.

200
00:19:27.761 --> 00:19:31.143
It had changed some . . . a whole lot.

201
00:19:31.143 --> 00:19:32.592
But it’s changed.

202
00:19:32.592 --> 00:19:36.458
So it's so much have passed and gone.

203
00:19:36.458 --> 00:19:37.424
Distant gone.

204
00:19:37.424 --> 00:19:38.874
It's left me.

205
00:19:38.874 --> 00:19:41.773
It just don't bother me anymore.

206
00:19:41.773 --> 00:19:43.223
It just gone.

207
00:19:43.223 --> 00:19:45.155
It just ain't there.

208
00:19:45.155 --> 00:19:49.021
I don't know, but it just ain't there.

209
00:19:49.021 --> 00:19:53.853
I know it's lots more, but it just ain't there.

210
00:19:53.853 --> 00:19:59.651
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about . . . So you missed a lot of school.

211
00:19:59.651 --> 00:20:04.000
But you did go to school in Mossville?

212
00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:04.442
<v PRATER>Yes.

213
00:20:04.442 --> 00:20:07.536
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what grade you started?

214
00:20:07.536 --> 00:20:14.609
<v PRATER>Well back then it was probably first . . . primer grade because . . . They called it primer, I think.

215
00:20:14.609 --> 00:20:20.797
Maybe you go primer to first grade, then second grade, third grade, like that.

216
00:20:20.797 --> 00:20:28.754
And well, like I said, the school I was in, it was just two rooms . . . by a partition.

217
00:20:28.754 --> 00:20:36.710
But it had, right through the middle, it had two big . . . I don't know what you call them.

218
00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:42.014
But anyway they could just raise them up and they roll up.

219
00:20:42.014 --> 00:20:45.109
One room here and one room there.

220
00:20:45.109 --> 00:20:46.877
The only two teachers.

221
00:20:46.877 --> 00:20:50.855
One in this one and one in that one.

222
00:20:50.855 --> 00:20:58.812
And the one in the . . . Had a lady in one side and a man in the other side.

223
00:20:58.812 --> 00:21:02.348
He was the principal and all you see.

224
00:21:02.348 --> 00:21:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember his name?

225
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:05.483
<v PRATER>Yes.

226
00:21:05.483 --> 00:21:08.382
I don't remember, but I can't . . .

227
00:21:08.382 --> 00:21:10.798
<v ARSENEAULT>You remember her name maybe?

228
00:21:10.798 --> 00:21:12.247
<v PRATER>Well Ms. [Laws?].

229
00:21:12.247 --> 00:21:16.596
Ms. Laws was my teacher that I went under.

230
00:21:16.596 --> 00:21:17.562
Ms. Laws.

231
00:21:17.562 --> 00:21:20.461
<v ARSENEAULT>And you had her for primer?

232
00:21:20.461 --> 00:21:25.292
<v PRATER>Yes, from primer on up to when I got out.

233
00:21:25.292 --> 00:21:27.225
She had about what?

234
00:21:27.225 --> 00:21:31.573
Two or three grades, all teaching one, two, three.

235
00:21:31.573 --> 00:21:32.539
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

236
00:21:32.539 --> 00:21:36.404
<v PRATER>About one, two, three, or four grades all . . .

237
00:21:36.404 --> 00:21:38.337
<v ARSENEAULT>All in one room.

238
00:21:38.337 --> 00:21:40.270
<v PRATER>In one room, yeah.

239
00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:40.753
And . . .

240
00:21:40.753 --> 00:21:46.551
<v ARSENEAULT>So she would teach the youngers and she would teach the older?

241
00:21:46.551 --> 00:21:47.034
<v PRATER>Olders.

242
00:21:47.034 --> 00:21:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

243
00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:48.536
<v PRATER>Yes.

244
00:21:48.536 --> 00:21:51.755
I didn't get into his room.

245
00:21:51.755 --> 00:21:55.509
But anyway, he . . . What was his . . . Professor . . .

246
00:21:55.509 --> 00:21:58.191
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he a black man?

247
00:21:58.191 --> 00:21:58.727
<v PRATER>Yeah.

248
00:21:58.727 --> 00:22:00.873
It was all black.

249
00:22:00.873 --> 00:22:03.018
What was his name?

250
00:22:03.018 --> 00:22:10.527
I know just as good as I know mine, but I can't recall it.

251
00:22:10.527 --> 00:22:14.818
<v ARSENEAULT>So do they call him Principal something or . . .?

252
00:22:14.818 --> 00:22:15.355
<v PRATER>Yes.

253
00:22:15.355 --> 00:22:16.427
<v ARSENEAULT>Or professor?

254
00:22:16.427 --> 00:22:19.109
<v PRATER>He was the principal, yes.

255
00:22:19.109 --> 00:22:21.255
<v ARSENEAULT>What'd he look like?

256
00:22:21.255 --> 00:22:21.791
Remember?

257
00:22:21.791 --> 00:22:25.009
<v PRATER>He was a real tall fellow.

258
00:22:25.009 --> 00:22:33.055
He was tall, about maybe a little bit taller than me as I can remember.

259
00:22:33.055 --> 00:22:37.345
I was young then and he was strict.

260
00:22:37.345 --> 00:22:38.955
He was strict.

261
00:22:38.955 --> 00:22:42.709
And I can't call his name now.

262
00:22:42.709 --> 00:22:44.318
It might come . . .

263
00:22:44.318 --> 00:22:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he from Mossville?

264
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:51.889
<v PRATER>No. I don't know where he was . . . But he stayed the whole session.

265
00:22:51.889 --> 00:22:54.897
<v ARSENEAULT>So he lived there while he was teaching?

266
00:22:54.897 --> 00:22:57.906
<v PRATER>Yes . . . No, he lived at my dad's mom.

267
00:22:57.906 --> 00:23:01.667
My grandmother that . . . His property was joined the school property.

268
00:23:01.667 --> 00:23:03.923
My grandfather on my dad's side.

269
00:23:03.923 --> 00:23:05.803
<v ARSENEAULT>So they put him up?

270
00:23:05.803 --> 00:23:06.179
<v PRATER>Yes.

271
00:23:06.179 --> 00:23:11.444
That's where he rented a room from my grandmother for the whole school session.

272
00:23:11.444 --> 00:23:15.205
Now he would leave . . . I think . . . I'm not for sure.

273
00:23:15.205 --> 00:23:17.838
It seemed like he was from Baton

274
00:23:17.838 --> 00:23:18.590
<c.p>Rouge [Louisiana].

275
00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:20.094
Which I'm not sure.

276
00:23:20.094 --> 00:23:24.231
But anyway, he would leave on the weekends and go home.

277
00:23:24.231 --> 00:23:25.735
But he would stay . . .

278
00:23:25.735 --> 00:23:27.991
<v ARSENEAULT>He would drive his car home?

279
00:23:27.991 --> 00:23:31.000
What kind of car did he drive?

280
00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:32.568
<v PRATER>I don't remember.

281
00:23:32.568 --> 00:23:34.135
I don't remember.

282
00:23:34.135 --> 00:23:34.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

283
00:23:34.658 --> 00:23:37.270
<v PRATER>Old car way back then.

284
00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:37.793
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

285
00:23:37.793 --> 00:23:40.928
<v PRATER>But anyway, he was real strict.

286
00:23:40.928 --> 00:23:41.973
Real strict.

287
00:23:41.973 --> 00:23:43.018
<v ARSENEAULT>How so?

288
00:23:43.018 --> 00:23:45.108
What do you remember?

289
00:23:45.108 --> 00:23:48.766
<v PRATER>Well back then . . . How he punished them.

290
00:23:48.766 --> 00:23:57.649
He had a strap and you'd hold your hand out and he was a big tall fellow.

291
00:23:57.649 --> 00:23:58.694
Heavy set.

292
00:23:58.694 --> 00:24:02.351
He probably weighed about 200 and something.

293
00:24:02.351 --> 00:24:04.441
He was real dark.

294
00:24:04.441 --> 00:24:14.892
He'd bring that strap down, and you had to hold your hand out, and he'd hit you in that hand.

295
00:24:14.892 --> 00:24:19.595
When he come down he didn't take no pity.

296
00:24:19.595 --> 00:24:23.252
He come down his jaw would shake.

297
00:24:23.252 --> 00:24:24.297
Big boys.

298
00:24:24.297 --> 00:24:29.000
It was mostly boys, he didn't bother the girls.

299
00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:30.568
It was different.

300
00:24:30.568 --> 00:24:37.360
For the boys though I have seen some of the hands swollen after.

301
00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:43.631
For a day or so the hand would be blue and swollen.

302
00:24:43.631 --> 00:25:00.351
<c.p>Nobody . . . The parents didn't bother him because they know it was . . . he had done something that they wasn't supposed to do because he wasn't going to do that for nothing like that.

303
00:25:00.351 --> 00:25:04.009
Back when I was coming up though,

304
00:25:04.009 --> 00:25:22.820
you could get whippings and stuff like that and it wasn't nobody going to go fight and all that about it because they know that you wasn't going to get no whipping unless you did something.

305
00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:24.910
So everybody understood that.

306
00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:27.000
You did something.

307
00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:35.452
Because sometimes you'd get chastised, somebody like that, and next two or three days they might see that person,

308
00:25:35.452 --> 00:25:43.459
they say, "Well did Morris tell you I had to get a hold to him the other day?"

309
00:25:43.459 --> 00:25:44.794
Talk about it.

310
00:25:44.794 --> 00:25:51.021
When Dad come back he come say, "Son, what went on the other day?"

311
00:25:51.021 --> 00:25:55.470
"I don't know." [ . . .?] He said, "Well yeah you did something.

312
00:25:55.470 --> 00:26:00.363
So-and-so said he had to get on you the other day." [ . . .?].

313
00:26:00.363 --> 00:26:14.598
It's best if you get a whipping from your aunts or your grandfather or somebody like that just when you get home to tell it because they was going to know it.

314
00:26:14.598 --> 00:26:20.381
And so you going to get another whipping if you didn't tell it.

315
00:26:20.381 --> 00:26:22.605
So it made us better.

316
00:26:22.605 --> 00:26:26.164
I mean, I appreciate every bit I got.

317
00:26:26.164 --> 00:26:30.167
Now Mom, she was strict too and my dad.

318
00:26:30.167 --> 00:26:39.064
Because we wanted to go somewhere you go ask Dad he going to say, "Well what did your mom say?"

319
00:26:39.064 --> 00:26:44.847
"Well Mom said it's all right if you say we can go there."

320
00:26:44.847 --> 00:26:45.737
It's fine.

321
00:26:45.737 --> 00:26:51.964
We go to Mom she going to say, "Well what did your daddy say?"

322
00:26:51.964 --> 00:26:57.747
They was on agreement with whatever this one do or that one do.

323
00:26:57.747 --> 00:27:07.534
If she said no well you go to him and he going to ask you what she said and she said no,

324
00:27:07.534 --> 00:27:14.206
well it was no. But they didn't . . . So it was strict, but I enjoyed it.

325
00:27:14.206 --> 00:27:17.320
My mom, she whipped pretty good too.

326
00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:19.544
My dad [he hardly?] whipped.

327
00:27:19.544 --> 00:27:21.769
Get a whipping from him.

328
00:27:21.769 --> 00:27:25.772
But mom, we'd get one from her pretty often.

329
00:27:25.772 --> 00:27:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What sort of things got you . . . What sort of things got you whipped?

330
00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:33.466
<v PRATER>Well it depends.

331
00:27:33.466 --> 00:27:38.843
Sometimes me and my brother used to get into it sometimes.

332
00:27:38.843 --> 00:27:40.799
We'd get into it.

333
00:27:40.799 --> 00:27:42.265
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean fighting?

334
00:27:42.265 --> 00:27:43.731
Wrestling and stuff?

335
00:27:43.731 --> 00:27:44.220
<v PRATER>Yes.

336
00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:44.709
Sometimes.

337
00:27:44.709 --> 00:27:54.485
We'd get on to fighting a little bit and she'd holler and ask what went on, were we hollering nothing.

338
00:27:54.485 --> 00:27:55.951
Something like that.

339
00:27:55.951 --> 00:28:00.351
Then after a while he'd keep crying or something.

340
00:28:00.351 --> 00:28:11.593
It was always . . . I would get it because they were younger than me and they could say I did this I did that,

341
00:28:11.593 --> 00:28:15.015
which a lot of times I did,

342
00:28:15.015 --> 00:28:25.280
because I was the oldest and I'd make them mad or angry like that and before you know it . . . hit them,

343
00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:26.746
something like that.

344
00:28:26.746 --> 00:28:35.545
So it . . . I appreciate every whipping that I got because it made a good person out of me.

345
00:28:35.545 --> 00:28:37.989
I never been to jail.

346
00:28:37.989 --> 00:28:48.743
Never went to visit anybody in there because . . . it wasn't because something I didn't do they just didn't catch me doing it,

347
00:28:48.743 --> 00:28:50.209
something like that.

348
00:28:50.209 --> 00:28:53.631
But other than that it's all right.

349
00:28:53.631 --> 00:28:57.052
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever go back to school?

350
00:28:57.052 --> 00:28:59.496
<v PRATER>No, I didn't go back.

351
00:28:59.496 --> 00:29:07.806
After I got up some age on me . . . I just didn't seem like it had the time.

352
00:29:07.806 --> 00:29:09.761
I had the time,

353
00:29:09.761 --> 00:29:25.892
but after you get out and you get up so . . . Get up in age it's just . . . It's something that you just don't understand and you just . . . That's the way it was with me.

354
00:29:25.892 --> 00:29:30.291
I just say well . . . I just give it up.

355
00:29:30.291 --> 00:29:39.090
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember when they built . . . So they had the two-room school house with principal and Ms. Laws.

356
00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:39.578
<v PRATER>Yes.

357
00:29:39.578 --> 00:29:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Then when did that stop being?

358
00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.180
<v PRATER>I don't remember.

359
00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:44.573
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

360
00:29:44.573 --> 00:29:45.753
<v PRATER>I don't remember.

361
00:29:45.753 --> 00:29:46.933
I don't remember.

362
00:29:46.933 --> 00:29:49.685
That's what I say, I can't remember.

363
00:29:49.685 --> 00:29:58.730
But anyway, when school’s going on . . . Like I say now, the boys played on one side and the girls on the other side.

364
00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:03.449
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, so was it two sides the boys on one and girls . . .?

365
00:30:03.449 --> 00:30:03.843
<v PRATER>Right.

366
00:30:03.843 --> 00:30:04.236
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

367
00:30:04.236 --> 00:30:05.809
<v PRATER>They're out to play.

368
00:30:05.809 --> 00:30:07.382
Now at the school . . .

369
00:30:07.382 --> 00:30:08.955
<v ARSENEAULT>You can't play together?

370
00:30:08.955 --> 00:30:12.101
<v PRATER>No. In the school now it was mixed.

371
00:30:12.101 --> 00:30:13.281
Guys and girls.

372
00:30:13.281 --> 00:30:13.674
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

373
00:30:13.674 --> 00:30:19.966
<v PRATER>But they might have had the girls all on desks on that side of the room.

374
00:30:19.966 --> 00:30:20.753
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

375
00:30:20.753 --> 00:30:22.719
<v PRATER>And the boys over there.

376
00:30:22.719 --> 00:30:25.079
It wasn't like it is nowadays.

377
00:30:25.079 --> 00:30:35.303
Out playing, when you go out for recess like that, well all the boys is on this side and the girls is all on that side.

378
00:30:35.303 --> 00:30:42.775
We be playing ball, if your ball go over on that side you didn't go over to get it.

379
00:30:42.775 --> 00:30:46.315
Had a fence all the way around the school.

380
00:30:46.315 --> 00:30:48.674
All the way around the property.

381
00:30:48.674 --> 00:30:51.034
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember where it was?

382
00:30:51.034 --> 00:30:53.000
Like the general area?

383
00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:54.826
<v PRATER>It's right where the school is now.

384
00:30:54.826 --> 00:30:56.391
Where that school is in Mossville.

385
00:30:56.391 --> 00:30:56.652
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

386
00:30:56.652 --> 00:30:58.217
<v PRATER>Where they got the office now.

387
00:30:58.217 --> 00:30:59.261
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, the Sasol office.

388
00:30:59.261 --> 00:31:00.565
<v PRATER>Sasol office, that's the school.

389
00:31:00.565 --> 00:31:03.174
<v ARSENEAULT>So they tore that old building down and built it.

390
00:31:03.174 --> 00:31:03.957
<v PRATER>And built that.

391
00:31:03.957 --> 00:31:04.478
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

392
00:31:04.478 --> 00:31:05.000
Okay.

393
00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:06.461
<v PRATER>No, no, no it ain't.

394
00:31:06.461 --> 00:31:08.506
It's right there where the rec is.

395
00:31:08.506 --> 00:31:11.719
<v ARSENEAULT>So the old school used to be where the rec is?

396
00:31:11.719 --> 00:31:13.180
<v PRATER>Where the rec is, yes.

397
00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:16.101
<v ARSENEAULT>And they moved the new school a little ways down.

398
00:31:16.101 --> 00:31:16.685
<v PRATER>Yes, right.

399
00:31:16.685 --> 00:31:16.978
Right.

400
00:31:16.978 --> 00:31:17.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

401
00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:24.573
<v PRATER>Yes . . . No it's right where that rec is because see my grandfather lived in that property right at the woods right there from the rec.

402
00:31:24.573 --> 00:31:26.618
In the woods that's where my dad's . . .

403
00:31:26.618 --> 00:31:28.663
<v ARSENEAULT>So he sold it to the school?

404
00:31:28.663 --> 00:31:31.000
So he sold it to the rec?

405
00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:34.368
<v PRATER>I don't know how they got the school . . .

406
00:31:34.368 --> 00:31:36.053
<v ARSENEAULT>How they got the . . .

407
00:31:36.053 --> 00:31:37.316
<v PRATER>. . . that property there.

408
00:31:37.316 --> 00:31:38.579
I don't know.

409
00:31:38.579 --> 00:31:41.105
<v ARSENEAULT>Or he donated it or something.

410
00:31:41.105 --> 00:31:41.947
<v PRATER>Could have.

411
00:31:41.947 --> 00:31:43.211
I don't remember.

412
00:31:43.211 --> 00:31:46.158
But anyway, that's where the school was.

413
00:31:46.158 --> 00:31:52.895
And like I say, the boys are on this side and the girls on that side.

414
00:31:52.895 --> 00:32:00.053
If you're off playing ball your ball went over there you didn't go across to get it.

415
00:32:00.053 --> 00:32:13.526
If the girls didn't throw it back across you had to go inside to the teacher the principal or else Ms. Laws and get permission to go over there and get it.

416
00:32:13.526 --> 00:32:23.211
And they mostly walk out and watch you go and get your ball or get the girls to throw it over back over.

417
00:32:23.211 --> 00:32:27.000
It was no . . . You didn't cross the line.

418
00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:29.852
<v ARSENEAULT>What would happen if you did?

419
00:32:29.852 --> 00:32:31.754
<v PRATER>Well you got it.

420
00:32:31.754 --> 00:32:33.180
<v ARSENEAULT>Get the strap.

421
00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:34.607
<v PRATER>You got it.

422
00:32:34.607 --> 00:32:36.508
Yes, you got it.

423
00:32:36.508 --> 00:32:45.066
Like I said, the fence was all the way around and they had steps go over the fence.

424
00:32:45.066 --> 00:32:50.295
Had steps, wide steps from here to the wall over there.

425
00:32:50.295 --> 00:32:51.721
They step up

426
00:32:51.721 --> 00:32:53.623
<c.p>and over the fence.

427
00:32:53.623 --> 00:32:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was safe?

428
00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:05.827
<v PRATER>And then go back down that side and all that width going up from the fence up to the stool,

429
00:33:05.827 --> 00:33:09.757
well that was . . . You didn't cross that line.

430
00:33:09.757 --> 00:33:13.197
That was the boundary line right there.

431
00:33:13.197 --> 00:33:19.092
When recess was over all the boys they line up over here.

432
00:33:19.092 --> 00:33:20.566
Had two lines.

433
00:33:20.566 --> 00:33:24.497
One line it was girls on this side. [ . . .?].

434
00:33:24.497 --> 00:33:27.445
<v ARSENEAULT>How is the school day organized?

435
00:33:27.445 --> 00:33:35.798
Like what time did it start and did you say a pledge or anything in the morning?

436
00:33:35.798 --> 00:33:36.289
<v PRATER>Yes.

437
00:33:36.289 --> 00:33:37.272
Oh yes.

438
00:33:37.272 --> 00:33:39.728
Had that and a prayer.

439
00:33:39.728 --> 00:33:44.642
That was every morning you had that or a prayer.

440
00:33:44.642 --> 00:33:48.572
And then we had 10 o'clock and 12.

441
00:33:48.572 --> 00:33:52.994
Probably three or something like that you get out.

442
00:33:52.994 --> 00:33:54.960
I guess it's three.

443
00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:57.416
I can't remember now anyway.

444
00:33:57.416 --> 00:34:04.786
Had a recess in the morning you know it started about eight o'clock I think.

445
00:34:04.786 --> 00:34:07.243
Then recess probably 10 o'clock.

446
00:34:07.243 --> 00:34:12.647
Get out and two o'clock I think you get out again.

447
00:34:12.647 --> 00:34:14.121
I'm not sure.

448
00:34:14.121 --> 00:34:21.000
But it was I remember you'd have some play time during that time.

449
00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:29.842
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the games that you used to play?

450
00:34:29.842 --> 00:34:32.789
<v PRATER>Two or three games.

451
00:34:32.789 --> 00:34:35.000
We played ball.

452
00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:36.474
Played marbles.

453
00:34:36.474 --> 00:34:40.158
Shoot marbles and play ball.

454
00:34:40.158 --> 00:34:41.632
That was . . .

455
00:34:41.632 --> 00:34:43.842
<v ARSENEAULT>Like baseball or . . .?

456
00:34:43.842 --> 00:34:44.579
<v PRATER>Softball.

457
00:34:44.579 --> 00:34:47.526
That was about it.

458
00:34:47.526 --> 00:34:54.158
They didn't have nobody toys like they got now.

459
00:34:54.158 --> 00:35:12.579
When my time coming up we only got toys during Christmas time we got a toy and an apple and orange and a peppermint stick.

460
00:35:12.579 --> 00:35:17.000
Yeah that was for Christmas.

461
00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:19.040
<v ARSENEAULT>The good old days.

462
00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:21.589
<v PRATER>Yeah that's what I said.

463
00:35:21.589 --> 00:35:23.629
It's . . . We enjoyed it.

464
00:35:23.629 --> 00:35:30.767
We didn't know anything else, but that was it so we just enjoyed it.

465
00:35:30.767 --> 00:35:38.926
Now they got so much that children . . . They want so much and everything's so expensive now.

466
00:35:38.926 --> 00:35:52.183
When I was coming up everything was . . . You could take say a nickel and go to the store and buy a bag of candy with it.

467
00:35:52.183 --> 00:35:53.713
For a nickel.

468
00:35:53.713 --> 00:35:59.832
A penny you could go and had certain candy you could get.

469
00:35:59.832 --> 00:36:10.030
You could get a penny worth of candy and you get four or five pieces of candy for a penny.

470
00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:18.188
Me and my brother after we got up a little age I started working or something.

471
00:36:18.188 --> 00:36:23.287
Doing a little work and I bought me a bicycle.

472
00:36:23.287 --> 00:36:38.584
Second hand bicycle from . . . Well Butch mama was working for a lady there in Maplewood and they had a bicycle and that's where I learned . . . found out about the bicycle.

473
00:36:38.584 --> 00:36:42.663
Fifteen dollars I gave them for the bicycle.

474
00:36:42.663 --> 00:36:52.351
We used to go to the picture show then ride to Westlake for the picture show on Saturday evening.

475
00:36:52.351 --> 00:36:55.411
We'd go down there and in . . .

476
00:36:55.411 --> 00:37:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the name of the theater?

477
00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.691
<v PRATER>No, I guess I don't.

478
00:37:02.691 --> 00:37:05.381
<v ARSENEAULT>The [Midway?] was that one?

479
00:37:05.381 --> 00:37:07.534
<v PRATER>No, no it's Westlake.

480
00:37:07.534 --> 00:37:09.148
I don't remember . . .

481
00:37:09.148 --> 00:37:09.686
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

482
00:37:09.686 --> 00:37:15.605
<v PRATER>What the name of it was, no it was in Westlake.

483
00:37:15.605 --> 00:37:30.673
A little picture show there. [ . . .?] Braxton and Rigmaidens and [LeDoux?] we'd ride about . . . That old bicycle we'd ride down to Westlake on Saturday evenings and see the show.

484
00:37:30.673 --> 00:37:42.511
We could get in the show for . . . Well what happened there we'd go ask . . . I hadn't started work when we first started.

485
00:37:42.511 --> 00:37:50.045
But anyway, we'd go ask Dad for some money to go to the show.

486
00:37:50.045 --> 00:37:52.197
It took fifty cents.

487
00:37:52.197 --> 00:37:58.117
He would groan a while before he'd get at fifty cents.

488
00:37:58.117 --> 00:38:04.036
So twenty-five cents for me and twenty-five cents for my brother.

489
00:38:04.036 --> 00:38:16.951
And we could go see the show and get us a big box of popcorn for twenty-five cents and see the picture show too.

490
00:38:16.951 --> 00:38:22.332
We'd go down this and see [ . . .?] Cassidy and [Ashley Road?].

491
00:38:22.332 --> 00:38:26.637
And then we ride on back to Mossville.

492
00:38:26.637 --> 00:38:30.404
See the show; go back to Mossville.

493
00:38:30.404 --> 00:38:32.556
It has changed now.

494
00:38:32.556 --> 00:38:39.552
I don't know how much it cost to go to the show now.

495
00:38:39.552 --> 00:38:41.704
<v ARSENEAULT>A good thirty dollars.

496
00:38:41.704 --> 00:38:43.318
I'm telling you.

497
00:38:43.318 --> 00:38:44.395
<v PRATER>Oh boy.

498
00:38:44.395 --> 00:38:46.009
<v ARSENEAULT>I’m telling you.

499
00:38:46.009 --> 00:38:54.619
<v PRATER>We could go to the picture show for twenty-five cents and get a box of popcorn.

500
00:38:54.619 --> 00:38:56.771
<v ARSENEAULT>You and your brother.

501
00:38:56.771 --> 00:39:00.000
What was your brother's name?

502
00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:00.558
<v PRATER>Clayton.

503
00:39:00.558 --> 00:39:01.674
Clayton, yes.

504
00:39:01.674 --> 00:39:04.465
<v ARSENEAULT>Is he still alive today?

505
00:39:04.465 --> 00:39:06.140
<v PRATER>No, he's dead.

506
00:39:06.140 --> 00:39:06.698
Yes.

507
00:39:06.698 --> 00:39:09.488
He was right under me.

508
00:39:09.488 --> 00:39:17.860
We'd ride a bicycle down there and we'd get together and go every Saturday evening.

509
00:39:17.860 --> 00:39:20.651
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a segregated theater?

510
00:39:20.651 --> 00:39:21.209
<v PRATER>Yes.

511
00:39:21.209 --> 00:39:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y'all sit?

512
00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:33.349
<v PRATER>We was on the bottom first, but they got so that they started throwing stuff down on the blacks.

513
00:39:33.349 --> 00:39:38.762
The whites when they was upstairs we was on the bottom.

514
00:39:38.762 --> 00:39:43.683
They started throwing stuff down so then they switched it.

515
00:39:43.683 --> 00:39:55.492
Put blacks up and the white down because blacks would hardly bother them, throw nothing on them, because they understood what was going on

516
00:39:55.492 --> 00:39:57.952
<c.p>so they didn't bother them.

517
00:39:57.952 --> 00:40:01.889
But so it made a difference you see.

518
00:40:01.889 --> 00:40:07.302
They could do whatever they mostly wanted to, but you couldn't.

519
00:40:07.302 --> 00:40:10.746
You could, but you'd be in trouble.

520
00:40:10.746 --> 00:40:11.730
You know?

521
00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:16.159
So we just knowed to stay in our place.

522
00:40:16.159 --> 00:40:24.032
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember your first theater where you didn't have to sit in a designated area?

523
00:40:24.032 --> 00:40:26.000
Your first movie?

524
00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:38.420
<v PRATER>No, because after I left from Mossville and went in the service, well then I didn't hardly take in any shows after that.

525
00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:42.740
<v ARSENEAULT>You were getting married and you were busy.

526
00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:43.280
<v PRATER>Yes.

527
00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:50.300
I went in the service in '51 so I come out in '53.

528
00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:55.700
So I got married six months after I got out.

529
00:40:55.700 --> 00:41:08.120
Well me and the wife, we went a few times, and we still used to go after the girls got up some age.

530
00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:11.360
We would go to the drive-in.

531
00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:17.300
That was on the Old Spanish Trail . . . No, on [Highway] 90.

532
00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:20.000
That was on 90.

533
00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:22.687
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh there was one in Mossville?

534
00:41:22.687 --> 00:41:24.030
<v PRATER>On 90, yes.

535
00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:25.821
Right back . . . Let's see.

536
00:41:25.821 --> 00:41:26.269
<v ARSENEAULT>Ninety.

537
00:41:26.269 --> 00:41:28.060
Is that Prater [Road]?

538
00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:36.567
<v PRATER>No, it was on 90, and see Prater leads from go across 90 and goes to Old Spanish Trail.

539
00:41:36.567 --> 00:41:37.463
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

540
00:41:37.463 --> 00:41:42.836
<v PRATER>See used to be that Old Spanish Trail where the school is.

541
00:41:42.836 --> 00:41:45.522
That was the Old Spanish Trail.

542
00:41:45.522 --> 00:41:50.000
I think they changed the name on it now.

543
00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:51.806
<v ARSENEAULT>It's Old Spanish Trail now.

544
00:41:51.806 --> 00:41:52.889
Then it's Burton.

545
00:41:52.889 --> 00:41:54.694
Then it's Old Spanish Trail.

546
00:41:54.694 --> 00:41:55.778
<v PRATER>Yes, it's Burton.

547
00:41:55.778 --> 00:41:59.028
Yes, it used to be all Old Spanish Trail.

548
00:41:59.028 --> 00:41:59.389
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

549
00:41:59.389 --> 00:42:00.472
<v PRATER>So but 90 . . .

550
00:42:00.472 --> 00:42:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It's parallel to Old Spanish Trail?

551
00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:03.349
<v PRATER>Yes.

552
00:42:03.349 --> 00:42:03.698
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

553
00:42:03.698 --> 00:42:06.140
It's how you get on the interstate.

554
00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:10.674
<v PRATER>Ninety is what . . . You probably come in on 90 across the I-10 Bridge.

555
00:42:10.674 --> 00:42:11.023
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

556
00:42:11.023 --> 00:42:12.070
<v PRATER>I-10, that's 90.

557
00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:13.814
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, I know that one.

558
00:42:13.814 --> 00:42:14.163
<v PRATER>Yes.

559
00:42:14.163 --> 00:42:14.512
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

560
00:42:14.512 --> 00:42:16.953
So the theater was on there somewhere?

561
00:42:16.953 --> 00:42:17.302
<v PRATER>Yes.

562
00:42:17.302 --> 00:42:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

563
00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:24.964
<v PRATER>It was right from Prater Road, right if you turn going east from Prater Road.

564
00:42:24.964 --> 00:42:31.000
It was about . . . I want to say about five or six hundred feet.

565
00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:37.500
It was about from here to when you go back out and turn right.

566
00:42:37.500 --> 00:42:42.143
It was about that far from Prater Road back east.

567
00:42:42.143 --> 00:42:44.929
That's where the drive-in theater was.

568
00:42:44.929 --> 00:42:46.786
So we'd go there.

569
00:42:46.786 --> 00:42:54.679
After that I didn't . . . We didn’t really go to . . . Me and my wife didn't go to shows.

570
00:42:54.679 --> 00:42:57.929
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know who owned the drive-in?

571
00:42:57.929 --> 00:43:03.036
<v PRATER>Wife would drive the kids to Lake Charles to the show.

572
00:43:03.036 --> 00:43:05.357
She’d take them over there.

573
00:43:05.357 --> 00:43:10.000
Drop them off and go back pick them up.

574
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:14.696
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who owned the drive-in on 90?

575
00:43:14.696 --> 00:43:15.217
<v PRATER>No.

576
00:43:15.217 --> 00:43:16.783
<v ARSENEAULT>Who worked there?

577
00:43:16.783 --> 00:43:26.174
<v PRATER>Seemed like . . . Yes, it was a couple fellows working around there . . . Let me see what was his name.

578
00:43:26.174 --> 00:43:29.826
He was just around there working around.

579
00:43:29.826 --> 00:43:34.522
He probably used to cleanup or around and about.

580
00:43:34.522 --> 00:43:38.696
Pick up paper and stuff around right there.

581
00:43:38.696 --> 00:43:41.826
Seemed like he was a lead.

582
00:43:41.826 --> 00:43:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do they have food at the drive-in?

583
00:43:46.000 --> 00:43:48.873
<v PRATER>Yes, they had probably popcorn.

584
00:43:48.873 --> 00:43:52.321
About all they had, I think.

585
00:43:52.321 --> 00:43:54.045
I'm not sure.

586
00:43:54.045 --> 00:43:56.343
They had little candy.

587
00:43:56.343 --> 00:44:02.664
Some kind of candy or something and popcorn at the drive-in.

588
00:44:02.664 --> 00:44:03.239
Yeah.

589
00:44:03.239 --> 00:44:06.112
So it's . . . it has changed.

590
00:44:06.112 --> 00:44:22.201
But Mossville it was a nice quiet place until I don't know before I went in the service there where we used to stay at that house there.

591
00:44:22.201 --> 00:44:24.500
We used to stay.

592
00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:28.522
They made a club out of it.

593
00:44:28.522 --> 00:44:30.246
Made a club.

594
00:44:30.246 --> 00:44:41.739
Fellow in Lake Charles, had a club over there so I don't know whether he bought that place or what.

595
00:44:41.739 --> 00:44:50.358
But anyway, they went in and they took all the . . . Gutted the rooms and all.

596
00:44:50.358 --> 00:44:52.082
Made one big . . .

597
00:44:52.082 --> 00:44:57.254
<v ARSENEAULT>So the house y'all rented when you were young?

598
00:44:57.254 --> 00:44:57.828
<v PRATER>Yes.

599
00:44:57.828 --> 00:45:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They turned it unto . . . What club was it?

600
00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:10.727
<v PRATER>After we moved another family moved in there and they had hogs and all that there.

601
00:45:10.727 --> 00:45:13.624
He had hogs there and all.

602
00:45:13.624 --> 00:45:20.385
But now the fellow that he sold it too . . . I don't know what happened.

603
00:45:20.385 --> 00:45:21.834
I don't remember.

604
00:45:21.834 --> 00:45:27.146
But anyway, they went inside and took all the rooms out.

605
00:45:27.146 --> 00:45:33.907
Just made one big open room and they made a club out of it.

606
00:45:33.907 --> 00:45:41.151
A fellow in Lake Charles had a club so he made a club out there.

607
00:45:41.151 --> 00:45:44.532
<v ARSENEAULT>So he had two or a couple?

608
00:45:44.532 --> 00:45:46.463
<v PRATER>Yes, he had two.

609
00:45:46.463 --> 00:45:47.912
<v ARSENEAULT>A couple around.

610
00:45:47.912 --> 00:45:48.878
<v PRATER>Two clubs.

611
00:45:48.878 --> 00:45:53.707
They used to . . . Clubs close up at a certain time.

612
00:45:53.707 --> 00:45:55.156
Maybe on Saturday.

613
00:45:55.156 --> 00:45:59.020
Maybe one or two o'clock morning they close.

614
00:45:59.020 --> 00:46:03.849
Well okay, a lot of them was at that club.

615
00:46:03.849 --> 00:46:13.024
They'd come out there at Mossville at that place and stay out there until the rest of the day.

616
00:46:13.024 --> 00:46:18.820
But anyway, I started going there before I went in the service.

617
00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:22.683
That' why I started going to that club.

618
00:46:22.683 --> 00:46:26.546
That was named The Wagon Wheel, I believe.

619
00:46:26.546 --> 00:46:28.961
Named that The Wagon Wheel.

620
00:46:28.961 --> 00:46:31.859
So I used to go there.

621
00:46:31.859 --> 00:46:32.341
In

622
00:46:32.341 --> 00:46:38.620
<c.p>Lake Charles I used to go to clubs over there in Lake Charles.

623
00:46:38.620 --> 00:46:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you hear any local musicians?

624
00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:47.417
<v PRATER>No. They was [ . . .?] when I started drinking the beer and stuff like that.

625
00:46:47.417 --> 00:46:49.083
It was all good.

626
00:46:49.083 --> 00:46:52.417
<v ARSENEAULT>You didn't know what you were listening to.

627
00:46:52.417 --> 00:46:54.083
It all sounded good.

628
00:46:54.083 --> 00:46:57.000
<v PRATER>It didn't matter. [laughs] It didn't matter.

629
00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:58.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any?

630
00:46:58.667 --> 00:47:02.000
Because I know they said The Paradise Club . . .

631
00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:02.417
<v PRATER>B.B.

632
00:47:02.417 --> 00:47:04.500
King was one of them.

633
00:47:04.500 --> 00:47:04.917
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

634
00:47:04.917 --> 00:47:06.167
You saw B.B.

635
00:47:06.167 --> 00:47:06.583
King?

636
00:47:06.583 --> 00:47:07.000
<v PRATER>Yes.

637
00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:07.417
B.B.

638
00:47:07.417 --> 00:47:12.833
King, and I had another one I can't think of his name now.

639
00:47:12.833 --> 00:47:19.500
But it made . . . They used to be at the clubs and play and sing like that.

640
00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:20.333
So I . . .

641
00:47:20.333 --> 00:47:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He's a legend.

642
00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:22.673
<v PRATER>Yes.

643
00:47:22.673 --> 00:47:23.347
B.B.

644
00:47:23.347 --> 00:47:26.714
King, he was one fellow.

645
00:47:26.714 --> 00:47:28.735
I'm telling you.

646
00:47:28.735 --> 00:47:31.429
Yes, and so they

647
00:47:31.429 --> 00:47:44.224
<c.p>made a club out there and then after . . . One fellow got killed out there at that club in there.

648
00:47:44.224 --> 00:47:46.918
<v ARSENEAULT>At The Wagon Wheel?

649
00:47:46.918 --> 00:47:48.939
<v PRATER>[Agrees] In Mossville.

650
00:47:48.939 --> 00:47:51.633
He got killed there.

651
00:47:51.633 --> 00:47:55.000
A fellow shot him.

652
00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:57.043
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know what happened?

653
00:47:57.043 --> 00:47:59.087
<v PRATER>Well I wasn't going then.

654
00:47:59.087 --> 00:48:03.174
That was after I come back out of the service.

655
00:48:03.174 --> 00:48:08.487
I didn't go . . . I didn't club after I come out of the service.

656
00:48:08.487 --> 00:48:12.574
I did all mine while I was in the service.

657
00:48:12.574 --> 00:48:16.252
Get married, well I just give it all up.

658
00:48:16.252 --> 00:48:18.296
But anyway, they say that.

659
00:48:18.296 --> 00:48:19.522
I don't know.

660
00:48:19.522 --> 00:48:21.974
But say he slapped a man.

661
00:48:21.974 --> 00:48:23.609
They was good friends.

662
00:48:23.609 --> 00:48:28.104
They'd been drinking all day and then they were good friends.

663
00:48:28.104 --> 00:48:31.783
They said he slapped him and he shot him.

664
00:48:31.783 --> 00:48:33.826
That's what they say now.

665
00:48:33.826 --> 00:48:35.052
<v ARSENEAULT>Crime of passion.

666
00:48:35.052 --> 00:48:36.278
<v PRATER>I wasn't there.

667
00:48:36.278 --> 00:48:39.139
I wasn't there, so I don't know.

668
00:48:39.139 --> 00:48:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who it was?

669
00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:50.182
<v PRATER>Harry [Mouton?] was the one that shot, and Clarence [LeDoux?] was the one that got shot.

670
00:48:50.182 --> 00:48:52.739
He was a young man.

671
00:48:52.739 --> 00:48:54.273
Tall, young fellow.

672
00:48:54.273 --> 00:48:57.852
About maybe my age, a little older.

673
00:48:57.852 --> 00:49:02.455
He was a young fellow when he got killed.

674
00:49:02.455 --> 00:49:07.568
Then later on another fellow got killed there, but he

675
00:49:07.568 --> 00:49:09.102
<c.p>got shot outside.

676
00:49:09.102 --> 00:49:12.170
Fellow went outside and shot him.

677
00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:14.727
<v ARSENEAULT>Outside of The Wagon Wheel?

678
00:49:14.727 --> 00:49:17.795
<v PRATER>Outside . . . He walk outside the place.

679
00:49:17.795 --> 00:49:25.977
But the first one got shot inside the place, and the other one got shot outside.

680
00:49:25.977 --> 00:49:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Outside.

681
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:30.636
<v PRATER>So then it closed up shop after that.

682
00:49:30.636 --> 00:49:31.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

683
00:49:31.091 --> 00:49:31.545
<v PRATER>Yes.

684
00:49:31.545 --> 00:49:38.364
It closed up because he went to the pen [penitentiary] the fellow did the shooting.

685
00:49:38.364 --> 00:49:42.000
He went to the pen so the place . . .

686
00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:42.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Bad press.

687
00:49:42.909 --> 00:49:47.000
<v PRATER>They closed it up, and that ended it there.

688
00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:50.636
<v ARSENEAULT>We heard about a murder in the 1930s.

689
00:49:50.636 --> 00:49:57.000
A young woman was killed by a man who was working the plants?

690
00:49:57.000 --> 00:49:58.636
<v PRATER>I don't remember him.

691
00:49:58.636 --> 00:49:59.864
In the '30s?

692
00:49:59.864 --> 00:50:01.091
<v ARSENEAULT>I believe so.

693
00:50:01.091 --> 00:50:02.318
Late '30s maybe.

694
00:50:02.318 --> 00:50:04.773
<v PRATER>I don't remember because I was . . .

695
00:50:04.773 --> 00:50:06.818
<v ARSENEAULT>Shot her in the street?

696
00:50:06.818 --> 00:50:08.045
<v PRATER>I can't remember.

697
00:50:08.045 --> 00:50:09.682
Where was it at?

698
00:50:09.682 --> 00:50:11.318
What was it now?

699
00:50:11.318 --> 00:50:16.227
<v ARSENEAULT>She was a fast . . . A loose woman I guess you could say.

700
00:50:16.227 --> 00:50:21.136
She would drive a corvette and I guess she had a reputation.

701
00:50:21.136 --> 00:50:28.909
And the man . . . She was seeing a man who came in from . . . I guess he was a seasonal worker.

702
00:50:28.909 --> 00:50:30.136
Something like that.

703
00:50:30.136 --> 00:50:35.864
And I guess he was angry that she was seeing more than just him.

704
00:50:35.864 --> 00:50:36.273
<v PRATER>Yes.

705
00:50:36.273 --> 00:50:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And I think he shot her in the street and then shot himself.

706
00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:42.981
<v PRATER>Oh okay.

707
00:50:42.981 --> 00:50:44.943
Okay, I remember that.

708
00:50:44.943 --> 00:50:46.906
Yes, that wasn't in . . .

709
00:50:46.906 --> 00:50:48.377
<v ARSENEAULT>That was later?

710
00:50:48.377 --> 00:50:50.340
<v PRATER>That was later, yes.

711
00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:55.736
That was . . . Yes that was another little club was out there.

712
00:50:55.736 --> 00:50:57.208
I remember now.

713
00:50:57.208 --> 00:51:00.642
There was another little club out there.

714
00:51:00.642 --> 00:51:02.113
Let me see.

715
00:51:02.113 --> 00:51:03.094
That was . . .

716
00:51:03.094 --> 00:51:04.566
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it?

717
00:51:04.566 --> 00:51:07.019
<v PRATER>It was west of Evergreen.

718
00:51:07.019 --> 00:51:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

719
00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:13.977
<v PRATER>Just west of Evergreen and not for . . . I don't know.

720
00:51:13.977 --> 00:51:15.172
<v ARSENEAULT>The pub?

721
00:51:15.172 --> 00:51:16.966
<v PRATER>I don't remember.

722
00:51:16.966 --> 00:51:18.161
<v ARSENEAULT>Joy Hill?

723
00:51:18.161 --> 00:51:23.540
<v PRATER>No, no. Joy Hill was way almost to Westlake.

724
00:51:23.540 --> 00:51:25.333
<v ARSENEAULT>The Cowboy Inn?

725
00:51:25.333 --> 00:51:27.126
<v PRATER>No . . . Cowboy Inn . . .

726
00:51:27.126 --> 00:51:28.920
<v ARSENEAULT>The Paradise Club?

727
00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:30.713
<v PRATER>That was Westlake.

728
00:51:30.713 --> 00:51:37.885
I don't remember what the name of that little place was there.

729
00:51:37.885 --> 00:51:39.678
But anyway, yeah.

730
00:51:39.678 --> 00:51:42.667
She did get killed there.

731
00:51:42.667 --> 00:51:46.851
Yeah that was probably in the '40s.

732
00:51:46.851 --> 00:51:47.448
Probably.

733
00:51:47.448 --> 00:51:49.241
She got killed.

734
00:51:49.241 --> 00:51:51.632
Her name was [Orally?].

735
00:51:51.632 --> 00:51:55.218
I think her name was Orally.

736
00:51:55.218 --> 00:51:57.011
I'm not sure . . .

737
00:51:57.011 --> 00:52:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she a Braxton?

738
00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.678
<v PRATER>No, she was . . . I don't know what she was.

739
00:52:04.678 --> 00:52:07.796
I can't remember what she was.

740
00:52:07.796 --> 00:52:10.395
But anyway, yeah she did.

741
00:52:10.395 --> 00:52:13.514
Yeah she did . . . She got shot.

742
00:52:13.514 --> 00:52:20.790
Shot with his shotgun, and it was concerning another man or something like that.

743
00:52:20.790 --> 00:52:29.106
I don't remember now because that was before I started going to the clubs and all.

744
00:52:29.106 --> 00:52:36.903
Well her mother stayed right across the road, and she had a big room there.

745
00:52:36.903 --> 00:52:40.541
She had a big [troller?] in there.

746
00:52:40.541 --> 00:52:47.298
And we could go in there because she sold soda waters and stuff.

747
00:52:47.298 --> 00:52:51.976
Her daughter had the club back across the road.

748
00:52:51.976 --> 00:52:56.134
So I used to go in that place.

749
00:52:56.134 --> 00:53:04.970
She had play records there and had a couple of slot machine in there you could dance.

750
00:53:04.970 --> 00:53:08.088
Had a big floor, could dance.

751
00:53:08.088 --> 00:53:12.246
But her daughter didn't get killed over there.

752
00:53:12.246 --> 00:53:21.602
He shot her and him and another fellow . . . He shot at another fellow but he . . . The other fellow,

753
00:53:21.602 --> 00:53:26.799
when he shot her, well the other fellow jumped him.

754
00:53:26.799 --> 00:53:33.036
Tried to get him because he was going to shoot him too.

755
00:53:33.036 --> 00:53:40.313
So they wrestled around there with the gun and they say . . . I wasn't there.

756
00:53:40.313 --> 00:53:49.669
But anyway, by them wrestling some kind of way he slung him all over the ditch and all.

757
00:53:49.669 --> 00:54:08.900
But anyway, when he got the gun away from him he hit him over the head with it and that bent the barrel and when he shot at him by the barrel being bent he missed him.

758
00:54:08.900 --> 00:54:11.498
Sprinkled him a little bit.

759
00:54:11.498 --> 00:54:16.696
And then the fellow he went on down and shot

760
00:54:16.696 --> 00:54:17.216
<c.p>himself.

761
00:54:17.216 --> 00:54:22.413
He went down where Jim Moss . . . That was the Moss.

762
00:54:22.413 --> 00:54:23.453
Jim Moss.

763
00:54:23.453 --> 00:54:34.888
Went down there and that's where he shot himself down there with . . . Jim Moss, that's the one had the little post office.

764
00:54:34.888 --> 00:54:44.243
That's where the mail used to go down with him and that's where he shot himself down there.

765
00:54:44.243 --> 00:54:47.362
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the post office?

766
00:54:47.362 --> 00:54:51.000
Did I ask you that already?

767
00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:52.097
<v PRATER>What’s that?

768
00:54:52.097 --> 00:54:55.389
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the post office?

769
00:54:55.389 --> 00:54:58.132
<v PRATER>Well that's what I said.

770
00:54:58.132 --> 00:55:03.069
They say they had a little . . . Well I mean,

771
00:55:03.069 --> 00:55:11.847
he had a little store and he just had a little place setup there where mail . . .

772
00:55:11.847 --> 00:55:14.590
<v ARSENEAULT>So he had like a . . .

773
00:55:14.590 --> 00:55:15.688
<v PRATER>Mail, yeah.

774
00:55:15.688 --> 00:55:18.431
<v ARSENEAULT>Where you could buy things.

775
00:55:18.431 --> 00:55:22.271
<v PRATER>Yes, he had a little . . . Wasn't much.

776
00:55:22.271 --> 00:55:25.014
Little candy and little cookies.

777
00:55:25.014 --> 00:55:25.562
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

778
00:55:25.562 --> 00:55:28.854
<v PRATER>And tobacco and stuff like that.

779
00:55:28.854 --> 00:55:29.951
I think.

780
00:55:29.951 --> 00:55:38.729
I don't remember the store because it’s out of business when I got up in age.

781
00:55:38.729 --> 00:55:46.410
So they say they used to drop the mail off there once a week.

782
00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:52.993
But many houses out there: the Braxtons, LaDous, Garretts, Rigmaidens, and Braxton.

783
00:55:52.993 --> 00:55:55.188
That was . . . And Perkins.

784
00:55:55.188 --> 00:56:00.125
That was about everything was in Mossville way back.

785
00:56:00.125 --> 00:56:02.319
It’s . . . Wasn't too many.

786
00:56:02.319 --> 00:56:04.514
Then started moving in.

787
00:56:04.514 --> 00:56:05.611
Moving in.

788
00:56:05.611 --> 00:56:06.160
So . . .

789
00:56:06.160 --> 00:56:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They moved in with the industry?

790
00:56:10.000 --> 00:56:13.757
<v PRATER>Well, no. Some of them did I guess.

791
00:56:13.757 --> 00:56:15.165
I don't remember.

792
00:56:15.165 --> 00:56:24.557
But yeah some of them did because people came in from I don't know where, but anyway, people came in.

793
00:56:24.557 --> 00:56:27.374
Some people that we didn't know.

794
00:56:27.374 --> 00:56:30.191
Way back then, didn't know them.

795
00:56:30.191 --> 00:56:31.600
They came in.

796
00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:36.296
The first plant was put in there was Cities Service.

797
00:56:36.296 --> 00:56:39.583
That was the first plant put in.

798
00:56:39.583 --> 00:56:42.400
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

799
00:56:42.400 --> 00:56:48.504
<v PRATER>Well that was something big because that was the first plant come in.

800
00:56:48.504 --> 00:56:52.730
Otherwise it wasn't anything in Westlake and Lake Charles.

801
00:56:52.730 --> 00:56:53.670
Like that.

802
00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:56.487
Wasn't nothing but a few stores.

803
00:56:56.487 --> 00:56:59.774
Westlake had, you now, a few stores.

804
00:56:59.774 --> 00:57:03.530
And Westlake and Sulphur had a few stores.

805
00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:05.878
Mossville had one little store.

806
00:57:05.878 --> 00:57:20.435
But anyway, when the plant come in I had people come in to build a plant and that was Kellogg I believe was the company that came and built Cities Service.

807
00:57:20.435 --> 00:57:20.904
<v ARSENEAULT>Kellogg?

808
00:57:20.904 --> 00:57:22.313
<v PRATER>Kellogg Construction, yes.

809
00:57:22.313 --> 00:57:28.887
They built Cities Service, and then after Cities Service then [Continental?] . . . Conoco down there.

810
00:57:28.887 --> 00:57:31.235
The next plant come in.

811
00:57:31.235 --> 00:57:36.400
But Mossville started picking up and the plants started coming in.

812
00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:38.278
People started coming in.

813
00:57:38.278 --> 00:57:42.035
Mossville started to grow a little bit then.

814
00:57:42.035 --> 00:57:44.852
<v ARSENEAULT>But what about the saw mills?

815
00:57:44.852 --> 00:57:51.896
What do you . . . Just going back a little bit because it's harder to find that.

816
00:57:51.896 --> 00:57:52.365
<v PRATER>Sawmill.

817
00:57:52.365 --> 00:57:55.183
Well they had one in Westlake.

818
00:57:55.183 --> 00:57:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there one in Mossville?

819
00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:00.462
<v PRATER>Well they had on in Mossville, yeah.

820
00:58:00.462 --> 00:58:02.571
They did have one in Mossville.

821
00:58:02.571 --> 00:58:06.440
It was little old fellow right there on Evergreen, I think.

822
00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:08.198
Had one there on Evergreen.

823
00:58:08.198 --> 00:58:09.956
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember his name?

824
00:58:09.956 --> 00:58:11.714
<v PRATER>It was white had that . . .

825
00:58:11.714 --> 00:58:13.473
<v ARSENEAULT>It was a white man?

826
00:58:13.473 --> 00:58:13.824
<v PRATER>Yes.

827
00:58:13.824 --> 00:58:15.231
Ross Reeves, I believe.

828
00:58:15.231 --> 00:58:16.989
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he live in Mossville?

829
00:58:16.989 --> 00:58:17.341
<v PRATER>No.

830
00:58:17.341 --> 00:58:19.802
<v ARSENEAULT>He just owned the saw mill there?

831
00:58:19.802 --> 00:58:22.615
<v PRATER>He just brought his saw mill out there.

832
00:58:22.615 --> 00:58:26.835
See there was a small thing there worked three or four men.

833
00:58:26.835 --> 00:58:27.538
That's all.

834
00:58:27.538 --> 00:58:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he employee people in Mossville?

835
00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:30.347
<v PRATER>Yes.

836
00:58:30.347 --> 00:58:33.816
They most all . . . And it was right up there at . . .

837
00:58:33.816 --> 00:58:34.510
<v ARSENEAULT>By Evergreen?

838
00:58:34.510 --> 00:58:34.857
<v PRATER>Evergreen.

839
00:58:34.857 --> 00:58:36.592
Now it wasn't too far.

840
00:58:36.592 --> 00:58:40.061
You know, Sasol building that little plant on Evergreen now?

841
00:58:40.061 --> 00:58:43.184
He was just a few hundred feet past there.

842
00:58:43.184 --> 00:58:43.531
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

843
00:58:43.531 --> 00:58:45.959
<v PRATER>Before you get to that curve there.

844
00:58:45.959 --> 00:58:48.735
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, I know exactly what you're talking about.

845
00:58:48.735 --> 00:58:50.816
<v PRATER>Okay, it was right in there.

846
00:58:50.816 --> 00:58:52.204
Little small thing there.

847
00:58:52.204 --> 00:58:54.633
You cut the timber right in there.

848
00:58:54.633 --> 00:58:56.367
<v ARSENEAULT>So he took local timber?

849
00:58:56.367 --> 00:58:57.408
<v PRATER>It was close.

850
00:58:57.408 --> 00:59:00.878
Yes, close right there where my dad worked right there.

851
00:59:00.878 --> 00:59:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Your dad worked at the one in Mossville?

852
00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:05.754
<v PRATER>My dad worked at . . . Yeah.

853
00:59:05.754 --> 00:59:06.105
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

854
00:59:06.105 --> 00:59:09.614
<v PRATER>Yeah and then he worked at the one in Westlake.

855
00:59:09.614 --> 00:59:09.965
<v ARSENEAULT>Westlake.

856
00:59:09.965 --> 00:59:13.123
<v PRATER>Well he used to ride his horse to Westlake.

857
00:59:13.123 --> 00:59:13.825
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh wow.

858
00:59:13.825 --> 00:59:17.333
<v PRATER>That was the only way they had to get around.

859
00:59:17.333 --> 00:59:18.035
Mostly horse.

860
00:59:18.035 --> 00:59:18.386
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

861
00:59:18.386 --> 00:59:18.737
<v PRATER>Wagons.

862
00:59:18.737 --> 00:59:24.000
Had a few of them had cars out there, but it wasn't very many.

863
00:59:24.000 --> 00:59:27.091
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who had cars?

864
00:59:27.091 --> 00:59:28.636
I'm just curious.

865
00:59:28.636 --> 00:59:32.242
I'm sure that was a big deal.

866
00:59:32.242 --> 00:59:36.879
<v PRATER>Had . . . Rigmaiden had a car. [Garrett?] had a car.

867
00:59:36.879 --> 00:59:38.424
And who else?

868
00:59:38.424 --> 00:59:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>All the shop owners?

869
00:59:41.000 --> 00:59:43.375
<v PRATER>Johnson had a car.

870
00:59:43.375 --> 00:59:45.750
That was about it.

871
00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:52.281
Yeah maybe one or two Braxtons might have had a car.

872
00:59:52.281 --> 00:59:55.844
It wasn't many cars out there.

873
00:59:55.844 --> 00:59:56.438
Wagon.

874
00:59:56.438 --> 01:00:04.156
My dad had a horse then after that he got him a wagon.

875
01:00:04.156 --> 01:00:07.719
<v ARSENEAULT>For all thirteen of y'all huh?

876
01:00:07.719 --> 01:00:11.875
Did y'all all fit in the wagon?

877
01:00:11.875 --> 01:00:12.469
<v PRATER>Yes.

878
01:00:12.469 --> 01:00:15.438
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it thirteen or eleven?

879
01:00:15.438 --> 01:00:17.812
<v PRATER>It was eleven kids.

880
01:00:17.812 --> 01:00:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sorry.

881
01:00:19.000 --> 01:00:23.588
<v PRATER>But I didn't hardly ride in the wagon.

882
01:00:23.588 --> 01:00:34.485
Not because . . . He didn't get the wagon until after I'd started working around like that, so I didn't go.

883
01:00:34.485 --> 01:00:45.956
After he got the wagon ride the horse and I used to ride the horse around and about like that.

884
01:00:45.956 --> 01:00:54.559
If I had to go to Westlake or something like that, I’d ride the horse.

885
01:00:54.559 --> 01:01:01.441
And he'd ride down there every morning and ride back every evening.

886
01:01:01.441 --> 01:01:08.324
Sunday . . . He worked mostly Saturday, but Sunday, that was his off day.

887
01:01:08.324 --> 01:01:11.765
But they would ride the horses.

888
01:01:11.765 --> 01:01:18.647
They'd gather up a bunch of men together from Sulphur, Westlake, Mossville.

889
01:01:18.647 --> 01:01:32.412
The ones had horses they'd gather up and have a lot of loose horses, wild horses, way on up in the woods up there.

890
01:01:32.412 --> 01:01:37.000
They'd ride running horses and catch them.

891
01:01:37.000 --> 01:01:40.762
<v ARSENEAULT>Who’s . . . How did the horses get wild in the woods?

892
01:01:40.762 --> 01:01:46.030
<v PRATER>They just . . . Horses just turn loose and horses have colts and keep . . . Loose horses.

893
01:01:46.030 --> 01:01:50.168
They had a lot of horses all from Mossville on up.

894
01:01:50.168 --> 01:01:52.426
Going up north like that way.

895
01:01:52.426 --> 01:01:55.059
See where Sasol is coming up through?

896
01:01:55.059 --> 01:01:58.822
All in there is where there used to be horses.

897
01:01:58.822 --> 01:02:01.455
That used to be nothing but woods.

898
01:02:01.455 --> 01:02:02.208
Just woods.

899
01:02:02.208 --> 01:02:03.713
No houses or nothing.

900
01:02:03.713 --> 01:02:05.970
<v ARSENEAULT>The horses lived in the woods?

901
01:02:05.970 --> 01:02:06.723
<v PRATER>Oh yes.

902
01:02:06.723 --> 01:02:07.475
Wild horses.

903
01:02:07.475 --> 01:02:08.604
They was wild.

904
01:02:08.604 --> 01:02:09.356
They'd ride.

905
01:02:09.356 --> 01:02:11.614
They'd run them all day long.

906
01:02:11.614 --> 01:02:12.366
Catch them.

907
01:02:12.366 --> 01:02:12.743
Ride.

908
01:02:12.743 --> 01:02:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they catch them?

909
01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:16.286
<v PRATER>They'd rope them.

910
01:02:16.286 --> 01:02:16.714
Rope.

911
01:02:16.714 --> 01:02:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And then what?

912
01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:18.857
<v PRATER>Ride them.

913
01:02:18.857 --> 01:02:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And then the horses . . .

914
01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:22.328
<v PRATER>Let them loose.

915
01:02:22.328 --> 01:02:24.541
They took some of them.

916
01:02:24.541 --> 01:02:28.967
Both white and black used to ride together like that.

917
01:02:28.967 --> 01:02:29.852
<v ARSENEAULT>They cowboys?

918
01:02:29.852 --> 01:02:35.607
<v PRATER>And sometimes they would take the ones that they were catching they want.

919
01:02:35.607 --> 01:02:38.705
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever catch a wild horse?

920
01:02:38.705 --> 01:02:40.918
<v PRATER>I didn't ride for that.

921
01:02:40.918 --> 01:02:47.557
That was . . . They'd ride . . . We . . . See I was coming up I didn't see Dad much.

922
01:02:47.557 --> 01:02:50.656
I saw him but it wasn't like . . .

923
01:02:50.656 --> 01:02:51.984
<v ARSENEAULT>He was working?

924
01:02:51.984 --> 01:02:54.639
<v PRATER>Like the lady kids coming on.

925
01:02:54.639 --> 01:03:00.836
He would be around with them because he would leave early in the morning.

926
01:03:00.836 --> 01:03:05.262
Maybe almost dark when he get back in the evening.

927
01:03:05.262 --> 01:03:09.689
Half a day Saturday and Sunday he was riding horses.

928
01:03:09.689 --> 01:03:12.787
That was every Sunday he'd ride horses.

929
01:03:12.787 --> 01:03:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's how he relaxed?

930
01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.200
<v PRATER>That was they sport.

931
01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:18.300
Ride horses.

932
01:03:18.300 --> 01:03:23.800
Then after Cities Service built then Firestone built down there.

933
01:03:23.800 --> 01:03:26.000
That's a plant too.

934
01:03:26.000 --> 01:03:27.100
Rubber plant.

935
01:03:27.100 --> 01:03:34.800
That Firestone is a rubber plant, and Cities Service was a gas plant there.

936
01:03:34.800 --> 01:03:39.200
But anyway, he got on then at Firestone.

937
01:03:39.200 --> 01:03:40.850
My dad did.

938
01:03:40.850 --> 01:03:42.500
Worked at Firestone.

939
01:03:42.500 --> 01:03:52.400
Well the he would just work horses from seven in the morning to maybe three in the evening.

940
01:03:52.400 --> 01:03:57.900
So he'd be home a little earlier in the evening.

941
01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:04.500
But I done got up through age then I didn't, you now.

942
01:04:04.500 --> 01:04:06.150
<v ARSENEAULT>You were older?

943
01:04:06.150 --> 01:04:08.900
<v PRATER>Yes, I was older then.

944
01:04:08.900 --> 01:04:12.200
So he would be off then.

945
01:04:12.200 --> 01:04:14.950
And then he retired then.

946
01:04:14.950 --> 01:04:24.300
He'd be around home then, but I was gone then so I didn't have too much time.

947
01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:32.000
I had time with him, but not like the last kids coming along there.

948
01:04:32.000 --> 01:04:34.750
They [were him around there?].

949
01:04:34.750 --> 01:04:38.600
He got him a car then after . . .

950
01:04:38.600 --> 01:04:41.350
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his first car?

951
01:04:41.350 --> 01:04:43.000
<v PRATER>A little Ford.

952
01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:47.400
I don't remember what model it was now.

953
01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:49.050
A 40-something model

954
01:04:49.050 --> 01:04:50.150
<c.p>Ford, yeah.

955
01:04:50.150 --> 01:04:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he let y'all drive it?

956
01:04:54.000 --> 01:04:56.976
<v PRATER>[Disagrees] I didn't drive it.

957
01:04:56.976 --> 01:04:57.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

958
01:04:57.571 --> 01:04:59.952
<v PRATER>I didn't drive it.

959
01:04:59.952 --> 01:05:11.857
Well his brother had bought it then his brother bought him another one, so he sold that to my dad.

960
01:05:11.857 --> 01:05:17.214
I bought . . . My first car was an old Oldsmobile.

961
01:05:17.214 --> 01:05:19.000
A '30 . . .

962
01:05:19.000 --> 01:05:22.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh they don't even make those anymore, do they?

963
01:05:22.600 --> 01:05:23.800
<v PRATER>A '38 . . . Mobile.

964
01:05:23.800 --> 01:05:24.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

965
01:05:24.200 --> 01:05:26.200
Did it have air conditioning?

966
01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:27.000
<v PRATER>No. [laughs]

967
01:05:27.000 --> 01:05:29.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to crank it?

968
01:05:29.400 --> 01:05:31.000
<v PRATER>No, you started batteries.

969
01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:32.600
A battery started it.

970
01:05:32.600 --> 01:05:37.000
I had that when I went in the service in '51.

971
01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What color was it?

972
01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:39.470
<v PRATER>Black.

973
01:05:39.470 --> 01:05:40.409
Yeah, black.

974
01:05:40.409 --> 01:05:42.758
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it have a radio?

975
01:05:42.758 --> 01:05:45.106
<v PRATER>Yes, I think it did.

976
01:05:45.106 --> 01:05:46.985
I ain't for sure.

977
01:05:46.985 --> 01:05:48.394
I forgot now.

978
01:05:48.394 --> 01:05:49.803
I don't remember.

979
01:05:49.803 --> 01:05:52.621
No, I don't think it did.

980
01:05:52.621 --> 01:05:57.318
Not that one, no. No I hadn't gotten married then.

981
01:05:57.318 --> 01:06:01.545
I had that when I went in the service.

982
01:06:01.545 --> 01:06:03.424
Well I wrecked it.

983
01:06:03.424 --> 01:06:04.833
I wrecked it.

984
01:06:04.833 --> 01:06:06.712
<v ARSENEAULT>Where'd you wreck it?

985
01:06:06.712 --> 01:06:07.652
<v PRATER>In Westlake.

986
01:06:07.652 --> 01:06:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Going to the club?

987
01:06:10.000 --> 01:06:11.988
<v PRATER>No, I was coming from.

988
01:06:11.988 --> 01:06:13.578
<v ARSENEAULT>Coming from the club?

989
01:06:13.578 --> 01:06:20.733
<v PRATER>I was on my way home and it was around, I don't know, two o'clock in the morning.

990
01:06:20.733 --> 01:06:21.130
Something.

991
01:06:21.130 --> 01:06:30.671
I don't remember just I remember stopping for a red light there in Westlake and when I left that I don't remember no more.

992
01:06:30.671 --> 01:06:32.261
<v ARSENEAULT>How'd they find you?

993
01:06:32.261 --> 01:06:37.031
<v PRATER>They had a little club right down from where I turned at.

994
01:06:37.031 --> 01:06:43.391
Where I turned at and went down a road where there was a little club there.

995
01:06:43.391 --> 01:06:52.534
Well it was a white and black with the black in one back room and the white . . . Well anyway, they heard the noise.

996
01:06:52.534 --> 01:06:54.522
It wasn't far from there.

997
01:06:54.522 --> 01:06:57.304
So couple fellows they come down there.

998
01:06:57.304 --> 01:06:58.497
Turn it over.

999
01:06:58.497 --> 01:07:00.087
I turn it over.

1000
01:07:00.087 --> 01:07:03.665
It flipped that car when they brought me home.

1001
01:07:03.665 --> 01:07:04.857
I don't remember.

1002
01:07:04.857 --> 01:07:06.845
But that was in December.

1003
01:07:06.845 --> 01:07:11.217
Well I was getting ready to leave for the service then.

1004
01:07:11.217 --> 01:07:14.000
That was my last big blowout.

1005
01:07:14.000 --> 01:07:15.171
<v ARSENEAULT>You knew you were about to . . .?

1006
01:07:15.171 --> 01:07:16.146
<v PRATER>I know I was gone.

1007
01:07:16.146 --> 01:07:17.317
<v ARSENEAULT>About to go to boot camp.

1008
01:07:17.317 --> 01:07:18.683
Not going to have for four years.

1009
01:07:18.683 --> 01:07:19.659
<v PRATER>I know I was gone.

1010
01:07:19.659 --> 01:07:20.439
That's what I did.

1011
01:07:20.439 --> 01:07:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Well you pay the price I think.

1012
01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:23.021
<v PRATER>I . . . Yes.

1013
01:07:23.021 --> 01:07:26.083
I had my last big breakdown.

1014
01:07:26.083 --> 01:07:28.634
<v ARSENEAULT>Went out with a bang.

1015
01:07:28.634 --> 01:07:29.145
<v PRATER>Yes.

1016
01:07:29.145 --> 01:07:30.166
Yes, so . . .

1017
01:07:30.166 --> 01:07:32.207
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you hurt bad?

1018
01:07:32.207 --> 01:07:33.738
<v PRATER>It didn't hurt.

1019
01:07:33.738 --> 01:07:36.290
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, the car was just . . .

1020
01:07:36.290 --> 01:07:36.800
<v PRATER>Yes [ . . .?].

1021
01:07:36.800 --> 01:07:37.310
Yes.

1022
01:07:37.310 --> 01:07:40.883
Got a fellow in there with me.

1023
01:07:40.883 --> 01:07:43.434
His arm got hurt though.

1024
01:07:43.434 --> 01:07:43.945
Yeah.

1025
01:07:43.945 --> 01:07:46.497
He had his arm out.

1026
01:07:46.497 --> 01:07:49.048
It turned on that side.

1027
01:07:49.048 --> 01:07:55.172
But then I left and it bent the old car all up.

1028
01:07:55.172 --> 01:07:57.214
Side and the back.

1029
01:07:57.214 --> 01:08:01.807
They sold it while I was in the service.

1030
01:08:01.807 --> 01:08:03.848
My mom sold it.

1031
01:08:03.848 --> 01:08:07.421
I didn't want it no more anyway.

1032
01:08:07.421 --> 01:08:13.545
Then when I come out, like I say, I got married then.

1033
01:08:13.545 --> 01:08:16.097
I bought another car then.

1034
01:08:16.097 --> 01:08:18.648
We bought a car then.

1035
01:08:18.648 --> 01:08:20.690
It was a Dodge.

1036
01:08:20.690 --> 01:08:23.752
‘41 Dodge, I believe it was.

1037
01:08:23.752 --> 01:08:31.407
That's what my wife . . . She learned how to drive on that . . . on it because it . . .

1038
01:08:31.407 --> 01:08:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It was a good one to learn on?

1039
01:08:36.000 --> 01:08:39.500
<v PRATER>Yes, it was . . . Well it was standard shift.

1040
01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:47.812
If you’re not used to driving a standard shift you got to know how to get off the clutch.

1041
01:08:47.812 --> 01:08:49.562
You don't need jump.

1042
01:08:49.562 --> 01:08:51.312
But anyway, it was . . .

1043
01:08:51.312 --> 01:08:53.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Who taught her how?

1044
01:08:53.062 --> 01:08:55.250
<c.p>PRATER She learned by herself.

1045
01:08:55.250 --> 01:08:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She got in?

1046
01:08:57.000 --> 01:09:02.937
<v PRATER>Well I mean I be running sometimes and she . . . But anyway,

1047
01:09:02.937 --> 01:09:14.810
it was . . . Well they call it a [fluid?] drive because you could shift it and when you get off it didn't jump.

1048
01:09:14.810 --> 01:09:19.127
You shift into first then second then high.

1049
01:09:19.127 --> 01:09:30.460
When you shift it down to high, well you could put your foot on the breaks don't mess with the clutch.

1050
01:09:30.460 --> 01:09:36.397
If it's on break it take off it's like automatic then.

1051
01:09:36.397 --> 01:09:48.270
It would change gears long as you didn't put your foot on the clutch it would change automatic like an automatic change.

1052
01:09:48.270 --> 01:09:49.889
First, second, high.

1053
01:09:49.889 --> 01:09:59.603
So that's what she learned how to drive because it didn't jump when you get off the clutch.

1054
01:09:59.603 --> 01:10:02.841
It just take on off slow.

1055
01:10:02.841 --> 01:10:05.540
<v ARSENEAULT>It was easier for her?

1056
01:10:05.540 --> 01:10:07.698
<v PRATER>She learned on there.

1057
01:10:07.698 --> 01:10:14.175
Then after that bought a . . . From that one then was automatic transition.

1058
01:10:14.175 --> 01:10:17.952
From then on she could drive them.

1059
01:10:17.952 --> 01:10:18.492
Yeah.

1060
01:10:18.492 --> 01:10:23.349
So it . . . I had several cars in my time.

1061
01:10:23.349 --> 01:10:26.048
I bought two new cars.

1062
01:10:26.048 --> 01:10:30.365
I remember I bought when I was younger.

1063
01:10:30.365 --> 01:10:34.143
My first new car was a '75.

1064
01:10:34.143 --> 01:10:35.222
Ford '75.

1065
01:10:35.222 --> 01:10:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What made you pick that one?

1066
01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:43.558
<v PRATER>Well it was a new car then.

1067
01:10:43.558 --> 01:10:49.419
A new car that was a '75 Model Ford.

1068
01:10:49.419 --> 01:10:51.372
I bought it.

1069
01:10:51.372 --> 01:10:57.233
I was working in Westlake then at a shipyard.

1070
01:10:57.233 --> 01:11:01.140
I was working there, doing welding

1071
01:11:01.140 --> 01:11:06.349
<c.p>there so I was making a little money.

1072
01:11:06.349 --> 01:11:08.953
So I bought that.

1073
01:11:08.953 --> 01:11:13.512
Getting classy then. [laughs] Yes, classy then.

1074
01:11:13.512 --> 01:11:20.023
Bought that in '75 and I kept it until '87.

1075
01:11:20.023 --> 01:11:21.977
I kept it.

1076
01:11:21.977 --> 01:11:29.791
But it was still in good shape when I traded it in.

1077
01:11:29.791 --> 01:11:30.442
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1078
01:11:30.442 --> 01:11:35.000
That's the year I was born.

1079
01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:42.682
<v PRATER>I trade that '75 in there and on '87 [ . . .?] Ford.

1080
01:11:42.682 --> 01:11:47.291
I kept it until about '90.

1081
01:11:47.291 --> 01:11:56.510
Probably '90 . . . What . . . Ninety . . . I forgot when I got rid of it,

1082
01:11:56.510 --> 01:12:02.656
but anyway I kept it in the '90s.

1083
01:12:02.656 --> 01:12:10.338
I bought me a truck then and sold that car.

1084
01:12:10.338 --> 01:12:15.715
I bought me a truck in 2011.

1085
01:12:15.715 --> 01:12:22.629
A 2011 truck and I sold the car then.

1086
01:12:22.629 --> 01:12:28.775
Well we went to Florida in the '87.

1087
01:12:28.775 --> 01:12:44.907
I retired in '92 . . . 1992 I retired and me and the wife and two of my daughters we went to Florida.

1088
01:12:44.907 --> 01:12:50.285
That was the first trip to Florida.

1089
01:12:50.285 --> 01:12:59.503
We stayed up there a week in Florida, and we drove it.

1090
01:12:59.503 --> 01:13:01.040
<v ARSENEAULT>The Oldsmobile?

1091
01:13:01.040 --> 01:13:02.576
<v PRATER>No, Chevrolet.

1092
01:13:02.576 --> 01:13:05.649
I mean, a Ford.

1093
01:13:05.649 --> 01:13:07.185
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, yes.

1094
01:13:07.185 --> 01:13:08.722
<v PRATER>The '87.

1095
01:13:08.722 --> 01:13:11.795
1987 Ford. [Crown Victoria?].

1096
01:13:11.795 --> 01:13:18.709
We drove it and went to Florida in '92.

1097
01:13:18.709 --> 01:13:27.159
Then we went back to Florida in . . . That one right there.

1098
01:13:27.159 --> 01:13:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>This is Disney World?

1099
01:13:31.000 --> 01:13:31.357
<v PRATER>Yes.

1100
01:13:31.357 --> 01:13:32.429
That right there?

1101
01:13:32.429 --> 01:13:32.786
Yes.

1102
01:13:32.786 --> 01:13:33.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Right here?

1103
01:13:33.500 --> 01:13:34.214
<v PRATER>No, that.

1104
01:13:34.214 --> 01:13:34.929
That one.

1105
01:13:34.929 --> 01:13:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>This one?

1106
01:13:36.000 --> 01:13:38.500
<v PRATER>That and we went to Florida in '90.

1107
01:13:38.500 --> 01:13:39.750
I believe that's '90.

1108
01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:40.688
<v ARSENEAULT>Is this 2009?

1109
01:13:40.688 --> 01:13:41.312
<v PRATER>2009, yes.

1110
01:13:41.312 --> 01:13:42.250
We went again.

1111
01:13:42.250 --> 01:13:43.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all go a lot?

1112
01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:46.312
<v PRATER>We went to Florida . . . I done made four trips.

1113
01:13:46.312 --> 01:13:48.812
My daughter they made about five or six.

1114
01:13:48.812 --> 01:13:49.438
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh wow.

1115
01:13:49.438 --> 01:13:50.375
<v PRATER>Seven trips there.

1116
01:13:50.375 --> 01:13:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1117
01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:54.500
<v PRATER>Me and the wife we went four . . .

1118
01:13:54.500 --> 01:13:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>This is y’all’s vacation spot?

1119
01:13:57.000 --> 01:13:58.500
<v PRATER>Yes, four times.

1120
01:13:58.500 --> 01:14:04.500
We went . . . I retired in '92 and then maybe went to Florida.

1121
01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:06.000
Stayed a week.

1122
01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:08.500
Then we went back again.

1123
01:14:08.500 --> 01:14:12.000
I don't remember what year that was.

1124
01:14:12.000 --> 01:14:15.000
Then we went back in 2009.

1125
01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:17.500
Then we went last . . .'15.

1126
01:14:17.500 --> 01:14:19.000
Last year.

1127
01:14:19.000 --> 01:14:19.650
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1128
01:14:19.650 --> 01:14:22.250
Oh that's Minnie Mouse.

1129
01:14:22.250 --> 01:14:26.150
<v PRATER>We went there last year, '15.

1130
01:14:26.150 --> 01:14:27.450
In May.

1131
01:14:27.450 --> 01:14:30.050
We went in May.

1132
01:14:30.050 --> 01:14:30.700
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1133
01:14:30.700 --> 01:14:41.100
Did your family . . . Growing up did y'all go on vacation with your mom and dad anywhere?

1134
01:14:41.100 --> 01:14:45.650
<v PRATER>No. No, we didn't take no trips.

1135
01:14:45.650 --> 01:14:55.400
Well it seemed like I remember a couple of trips we went to DeRidder [Louisiana].

1136
01:14:55.400 --> 01:14:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What's out there?

1137
01:14:58.000 --> 01:15:00.862
<v PRATER>My great grandfather lived in DeRidder.

1138
01:15:00.862 --> 01:15:04.200
So went up there a couple of

1139
01:15:04.200 --> 01:15:04.677
<c.p>times.

1140
01:15:04.677 --> 01:15:08.492
I can remember my . . . when I was young.

1141
01:15:08.492 --> 01:15:12.308
First trip we rode the train I think.

1142
01:15:12.308 --> 01:15:14.692
Rode the train to DeRidder.

1143
01:15:14.692 --> 01:15:18.031
It's stopped in Mossville at the crossing.

1144
01:15:18.031 --> 01:15:22.800
You know where Old Spanish Trail cross the railroad there?

1145
01:15:22.800 --> 01:15:26.138
That's where we caught it there. [laughs]

1146
01:15:26.138 --> 01:15:26.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Nice.

1147
01:15:26.615 --> 01:15:29.000
<v PRATER>Old train on up.

1148
01:15:29.000 --> 01:15:31.031
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a depot in Mossville?

1149
01:15:31.031 --> 01:15:36.446
<v PRATER>[Disagrees] You could just catch . . . We just caught the train it stop at the crossing there.

1150
01:15:36.446 --> 01:15:37.800
<v ARSENEAULT>You just hopped on.

1151
01:15:37.800 --> 01:15:40.508
<v PRATER>No, had to buy a ticket and all.

1152
01:15:40.508 --> 01:15:43.215
<v ARSENEAULT>But you just bought one on the train?

1153
01:15:43.215 --> 01:15:43.554
<v PRATER>Yes.

1154
01:15:43.554 --> 01:15:43.892
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1155
01:15:43.892 --> 01:15:44.231
<v PRATER>Yes.

1156
01:15:44.231 --> 01:15:46.262
Bought a ticket on the train.

1157
01:15:46.262 --> 01:15:49.308
It was an old, old thing way back then.

1158
01:15:49.308 --> 01:15:49.646
Old.

1159
01:15:49.646 --> 01:15:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Could sit down?

1160
01:15:51.000 --> 01:15:54.961
<v PRATER>It runs slow, but it stopped there.

1161
01:15:54.961 --> 01:16:02.882
We caught it right at the railroad there where it crossed over right there.

1162
01:16:02.882 --> 01:16:05.711
Went on up to DeRidder.

1163
01:16:05.711 --> 01:16:11.934
We rode it back and put us down right back there.

1164
01:16:11.934 --> 01:16:28.342
Then we went back and another time we went my . . . One of my mom's sister's husband had an old model eight truck that had a big bed on it.

1165
01:16:28.342 --> 01:16:35.132
We all went up there on [the 19th?] I think one year.

1166
01:16:35.132 --> 01:16:43.053
I remember that's the only time I remember we . . . family took . . . Anything like that.

1167
01:16:43.053 --> 01:16:56.632
Yeah, so it's all my going was after I got up and got married and left and then I'd go on around about then.

1168
01:16:56.632 --> 01:17:08.513
But other than that wasn't any money to go on no vacation and just didn't think about going on a vacation.

1169
01:17:08.513 --> 01:17:13.605
Wasn't anywhere really for them to go back then.

1170
01:17:13.605 --> 01:17:15.868
So it wasn't nothing.

1171
01:17:15.868 --> 01:17:16.434
Yeah.

1172
01:17:16.434 --> 01:17:20.395
Seemed like Mom we'd go to Christmas.

1173
01:17:20.395 --> 01:17:23.789
Go to Lake Charles for Christmas.

1174
01:17:23.789 --> 01:17:29.447
Buy clothes or shoes or something like that for Christmas.

1175
01:17:29.447 --> 01:17:33.974
But other than that, that was about it.

1176
01:17:33.974 --> 01:17:39.066
Through the summer there we didn't wear any shoes.

1177
01:17:39.066 --> 01:17:41.895
Barefooted all through the summer.

1178
01:17:41.895 --> 01:17:48.684
So we . . . Back then we'd just run and go through the woods.

1179
01:17:48.684 --> 01:17:51.513
Play and go on barefooted.

1180
01:17:51.513 --> 01:17:57.171
Now I can't walk around in the house barefooted hardly.

1181
01:17:57.171 --> 01:17:57.737
Hurts.

1182
01:17:57.737 --> 01:18:00.000
So yeah well . . .

1183
01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:02.047
<v ARSENEAULT>What about home remedies?

1184
01:18:02.047 --> 01:18:06.654
Did y'all have any home remedies y'all would use?

1185
01:18:06.654 --> 01:18:09.724
<v PRATER>Oh yes, they had home remedies.

1186
01:18:09.724 --> 01:18:13.819
They . . . Grandma, she'd go out there and get

1187
01:18:13.819 --> 01:18:15.866
<c.p>some kind of roots.

1188
01:18:15.866 --> 01:18:18.937
Make you a tea like that.

1189
01:18:18.937 --> 01:18:21.496
We wasn't that sick much.

1190
01:18:21.496 --> 01:18:28.661
Maybe a cold or something like that was about the only sickness we'd be.

1191
01:18:28.661 --> 01:18:33.780
Now [they'd give you black draught?] about once a week.

1192
01:18:33.780 --> 01:18:34.803
<v ARSENEAULT>What's that?

1193
01:18:34.803 --> 01:18:35.827
<v PRATER>Castor oil.

1194
01:18:35.827 --> 01:18:37.874
Castor oil, you know?

1195
01:18:37.874 --> 01:18:44.016
They give you that about once a week to clean you out.

1196
01:18:44.016 --> 01:18:49.646
Black draught well that was a . . . It was a powder then.

1197
01:18:49.646 --> 01:18:56.811
You can buy it in a liquid form but it was a powder then.

1198
01:18:56.811 --> 01:18:58.346
<v ARSENEAULT>What's it called?

1199
01:18:58.346 --> 01:18:59.370
<v PRATER>Black draught.

1200
01:18:59.370 --> 01:18:59.882
<v ARSENEAULT>Draught?

1201
01:18:59.882 --> 01:19:00.906
<v PRATER>Draught, yeah.

1202
01:19:00.906 --> 01:19:02.441
Black draught, yes.

1203
01:19:02.441 --> 01:19:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And you'd eat it?

1204
01:19:05.000 --> 01:19:13.081
<v PRATER>No, it just make a ball and then give it to you and get some water and swallow it down.

1205
01:19:13.081 --> 01:19:15.505
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it made out of?

1206
01:19:15.505 --> 01:19:16.717
<v PRATER>I don't know.

1207
01:19:16.717 --> 01:19:17.525
Nasty stuff.

1208
01:19:17.525 --> 01:19:19.141
<v ARSENEAULT>It didn't taste good?

1209
01:19:19.141 --> 01:19:20.354
It was gross?

1210
01:19:20.354 --> 01:19:21.566
<v PRATER>No, it didn't.

1211
01:19:21.566 --> 01:19:23.586
Had to clean you out.

1212
01:19:23.586 --> 01:19:26.818
Make your bowels move, clean you out there.

1213
01:19:26.818 --> 01:19:27.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Cleansing.

1214
01:19:27.222 --> 01:19:27.626
<v PRATER>Yes.

1215
01:19:27.626 --> 01:19:28.838
And lemon tea.

1216
01:19:28.838 --> 01:19:32.475
They give you lemon tea and stuff like that.

1217
01:19:32.475 --> 01:19:38.131
Other than that about all you had was a cold or something like that.

1218
01:19:38.131 --> 01:19:41.364
There wasn't no disease like there is now.

1219
01:19:41.364 --> 01:19:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what would they do for a cold?

1220
01:19:45.000 --> 01:19:48.254
<v PRATER>They'd get some kind of . . . Well Grandma,

1221
01:19:48.254 --> 01:19:56.623
she had something that she'd go out to get out there and come back, make you a tea.

1222
01:19:56.623 --> 01:20:01.272
Drink that; in a few days you was all right.

1223
01:20:01.272 --> 01:20:05.456
I remember my brother, he had what . . . malaria fever.

1224
01:20:05.456 --> 01:20:10.105
They called it malaria fever I think they called it.

1225
01:20:10.105 --> 01:20:13.360
But anyway, he had that one year.

1226
01:20:13.360 --> 01:20:16.614
He was serious sick with that fever.

1227
01:20:16.614 --> 01:20:20.798
And they had what they called them bitter weeds.

1228
01:20:20.798 --> 01:20:24.518
Whole big weed with yellow flowers on them.

1229
01:20:24.518 --> 01:20:27.307
They come up about so high.

1230
01:20:27.307 --> 01:20:31.491
They made us go pull a lot of them.

1231
01:20:31.491 --> 01:20:36.140
They had a big old wash pot on the outside.

1232
01:20:36.140 --> 01:20:42.649
A big old pot they put water in it and throwed wood around it.

1233
01:20:42.649 --> 01:20:44.044
Got it hot.

1234
01:20:44.044 --> 01:20:49.623
Got them bitter weeds and put them in that tubs back then.

1235
01:20:49.623 --> 01:20:54.737
They didn't have the tub like they got in the bathroom

1236
01:20:54.737 --> 01:20:55.202
<c.p>then.

1237
01:20:55.202 --> 01:20:57.526
They had this wash tub.

1238
01:20:57.526 --> 01:20:59.851
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a number three tub?

1239
01:20:59.851 --> 01:21:00.316
<v PRATER>Yes.

1240
01:21:00.316 --> 01:21:01.711
Number three tub.

1241
01:21:01.711 --> 01:21:09.149
They out them bitter weeds in there and poured that hot water in there on that.

1242
01:21:09.149 --> 01:21:20.772
They put a . . . Set a chair in there and set him in that chair and covered him up with a blanket and sweated him out.

1243
01:21:20.772 --> 01:21:24.491
In a few days he was all right.

1244
01:21:24.491 --> 01:21:29.140
I believe it was malaria fever they said he had.

1245
01:21:29.140 --> 01:21:31.000
I don't know.

1246
01:21:31.000 --> 01:21:33.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Had to sweat the fever out.

1247
01:21:33.750 --> 01:21:34.208
<v PRATER>Yes.

1248
01:21:34.208 --> 01:21:35.583
They did that.

1249
01:21:35.583 --> 01:21:40.167
And that was the only real sickness I can remember.

1250
01:21:40.167 --> 01:21:42.000
He was real sick.

1251
01:21:42.000 --> 01:21:43.375
Hot, hot fever.

1252
01:21:43.375 --> 01:21:47.958
Did that in a few days he was all right.

1253
01:21:47.958 --> 01:21:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about for cuts or bruises or anything like that?

1254
01:21:53.000 --> 01:21:56.601
<v PRATER>Yes.

1255
01:21:56.601 --> 01:22:21.809
They would put some turpentine on it.

1256
01:22:21.809 --> 01:22:47.017
We called it coal oil back then.

1257
01:22:47.017 --> 01:22:50.618
Kerosene.

1258
01:22:50.618 --> 01:23:08.624
<v ARSENEAULT>They poured kerosene on it?

1259
01:23:08.624 --> 01:23:30.230
<v PRATER>Yes, kerosene on it and turpentine.

1260
01:23:30.230 --> 01:24:56.657
Put that on there and then they go around the house and get some spider webs, put that on it and wrap it up.

1261
01:24:56.657 --> 01:25:25.466
I . . . spot on my foot there where I

1262
01:25:25.466 --> 01:27:09.899
<c.p>was out at the cow pen when I was smaller and see the [kin?] at around the side there and I climbed up on the . . . and slide down that.

1263
01:27:09.899 --> 01:27:35.107
Cut my foot all open under there.

1264
01:27:35.107 --> 01:28:07.517
Got around the house got that put on it.

1265
01:28:07.517 --> 01:28:18.320
Wrapped it up.

1266
01:28:18.320 --> 01:29:19.539
I was walking on my heel for a few days, but after that it got all right.

1267
01:29:19.539 --> 01:29:51.949
Didn't go to no doctor for nothing like that.

1268
01:29:51.949 --> 01:30:09.955
Just a cut like that.

1269
01:30:09.955 --> 01:31:11.174
Now, I don't remember breaking no bones or nothing so they didn't have no money to waste.

1270
01:31:11.174 --> 01:32:08.792
You cut your foot you didn't have no money to go to the doctor with that.

1271
01:32:08.792 --> 01:32:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You got to tough it out.

1272
01:32:34.000 --> 01:32:34.200
<v PRATER>Yes.

1273
01:32:34.033 --> 01:32:34.293
It'll be all right in a few days.

1274
01:32:34.293 --> 01:32:34.493
Stuck a nail.

1275
01:32:34.390 --> 01:32:34.618
I stuck a nail in my foot.

1276
01:32:34.618 --> 01:32:34.943
They put a little piece of fat meat on that.

1277
01:32:34.943 --> 01:32:35.143
Bacon.

1278
01:32:34.976 --> 01:32:35.176
Fat meat and bacon.

1279
01:32:35.106 --> 01:32:35.561
Slap piece . . . Cut a piece out, put it on there and wrap it up.

1280
01:32:35.561 --> 01:32:35.761
For a nail stick.

1281
01:32:35.691 --> 01:32:35.891
Pour some turpentine on it.

1282
01:32:35.854 --> 01:32:36.054
Go on.

1283
01:32:35.919 --> 01:32:36.119
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the turpentine for?

1284
01:32:36.081 --> 01:32:36.569
<v PRATER>That would take some of the soreness out of it and make it heal too.

1285
01:32:36.569 --> 01:32:36.769
Kerosene be the same thing.

1286
01:32:36.732 --> 01:32:37.122
Take some of the soreness out of it, and it heal up.

1287
01:32:37.122 --> 01:32:37.322
<v ARSENEAULT>Any other injuries?

1288
01:32:37.220 --> 01:32:37.420
Did you have any other?

1289
01:32:37.382 --> 01:32:37.582
<v PRATER>Yes.

1290
01:32:37.415 --> 01:32:38.390
After I got a pretty good age me and my brother . . . I think I told you they had two wood yards in Mossville because everybody out there mostly burned wood.

1291
01:32:38.390 --> 01:32:38.715
A lot of the white folks would come by too.

1292
01:32:38.715 --> 01:32:38.915
Fireplace wood and stove wood.

1293
01:32:38.878 --> 01:32:39.078
Stuff like that.

1294
01:32:38.976 --> 01:32:39.176
And I

1295
01:32:39.041 --> 01:32:39.241
<c.p>went to work for one there.

1296
01:32:39.236 --> 01:32:40.211
Me and my brother was going to go cut wood for him and I stick a crosscut saw and I stuck one of the teeth right there in my leg.

1297
01:32:40.211 --> 01:32:40.992
When I pulled my side and he didn't pull his side back it went in. [I kind of sat on it made it bow?].

1298
01:32:40.992 --> 01:32:41.317
When it bowed it bowed my way and it stuck.

1299
01:32:41.317 --> 01:32:41.517
One tooth stuck me in there.

1300
01:32:41.512 --> 01:32:41.740
Right in the front of my leg.

1301
01:32:41.740 --> 01:32:41.940
Hit right there.

1302
01:32:41.837 --> 01:32:42.037
In there.

1303
01:32:41.902 --> 01:32:42.325
Blood jumped up that high so that was the end of woodcutting there.

1304
01:32:42.325 --> 01:32:42.553
They done the same thing with it.

1305
01:32:42.553 --> 01:32:42.911
Poured coal oil and kerosene on it then wrapped it up.

1306
01:32:42.911 --> 01:32:43.236
Oh that was sewed up for a couple of days.

1307
01:32:43.236 --> 01:32:43.436
Real sore.

1308
01:32:43.301 --> 01:32:43.691
But you didn't get no shots or nothing for nothing like that.

1309
01:32:43.691 --> 01:32:44.016
But now you get cut you better get a shot.

1310
01:32:44.016 --> 01:32:44.216
Lock-jaw shot or something like that.

1311
01:32:44.211 --> 01:32:44.411
<v ARSENEAULT>Get a tetanus shot.

1312
01:32:44.341 --> 01:32:44.541
<v PRATER>Yes [ . . . ?].

1313
01:32:44.374 --> 01:32:44.574
Yes, lock-jaw shot.

1314
01:32:44.472 --> 01:32:44.672
Yes they had names for stuff.

1315
01:32:44.667 --> 01:32:44.867
It ain't like it is now.

1316
01:32:44.862 --> 01:32:45.122
They got the proper name for it now.

1317
01:32:45.122 --> 01:32:45.322
But it was back then . . . Yeah.

1318
01:32:45.317 --> 01:32:45.517
<v ARSENEAULT>Survival.

1319
01:32:45.350 --> 01:32:45.550
<v PRATER>Yes.

1320
01:32:45.382 --> 01:32:45.582
Yes, so . . . Yes, indeed.

1321
01:32:45.512 --> 01:32:45.772
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have any healers in the area?

1322
01:32:45.772 --> 01:32:45.972
<v PRATER>No, I don't remember any.

1323
01:32:45.935 --> 01:32:46.260
They practically all old folks . . . You know what I mean.

1324
01:32:46.260 --> 01:32:46.813
They had the remedies they would . . . You get that, but other than that, that was about it.

1325
01:32:46.813 --> 01:32:47.138
<v ARSENEAULT>Anybody born with a special gift or anything like that?

1326
01:32:47.138 --> 01:32:47.338
<v PRATER>No, not that I know of.

1327
01:32:47.333 --> 01:32:47.821
My grandmother on my mom's side there, you get a cut she could stop blood.

1328
01:32:47.821 --> 01:32:48.081
You bleeding or something she could stop it.

1329
01:32:48.081 --> 01:32:48.602
I don't know what she did, but she learned one of my brothers what to do.

1330
01:32:48.602 --> 01:32:48.802
How to stop it.

1331
01:32:48.732 --> 01:32:48.932
I know my mom,

1332
01:32:48.862 --> 01:32:50.260
she went and had some teeth pulled. [Up?] in the day and she spilled blood in the morning and she lost so much blood she kind of fainted out and my dad went got on the horse and went up and told her.

1333
01:32:50.260 --> 01:32:50.553
By the time he got back it had stopped.

1334
01:32:50.553 --> 01:32:50.753
Yeah, she had some teeth pulled.

1335
01:32:50.748 --> 01:32:51.041
She could spit out a mouth full of them.

1336
01:32:51.041 --> 01:32:51.241
Mostly was another on there.

1337
01:32:51.203 --> 01:32:51.431
She bled out so much she fainted.

1338
01:32:51.431 --> 01:32:51.724
She learnt one of my brothers what to do.

1339
01:32:51.724 --> 01:32:51.924
I don't know what it is.

1340
01:32:51.919 --> 01:32:52.179
She didn't learn me, so she learned him.

1341
01:32:52.179 --> 01:32:52.379
He can do it too, yes.

1342
01:32:52.374 --> 01:32:52.574
What they did I don't know.

1343
01:32:52.569 --> 01:32:52.769
What she do I don't know.

1344
01:32:52.764 --> 01:32:52.964
But it didn't take long.

1345
01:32:52.927 --> 01:32:53.127
It just . . . Yes.

1346
01:32:53.024 --> 01:32:53.512
I wish he would learn somebody else, because he's getting a little age on him.

1347
01:32:53.512 --> 01:32:53.870
Have somebody in the family kind of know what to do.

1348
01:32:53.870 --> 01:32:54.070
Something like that.

1349
01:32:53.967 --> 01:32:54.228
<v ARSENEAULT>So she just passed it on to him?

1350
01:32:54.228 --> 01:32:54.428
<v PRATER>Yes.

1351
01:32:54.260 --> 01:32:54.650
She could still do it but she passed it on to him.

1352
01:32:54.650 --> 01:32:54.976
She learn him, but what it is I don't know.

1353
01:32:54.976 --> 01:32:55.268
I didn't ask him, and I never asked her.

1354
01:32:55.268 --> 01:32:55.951
When I talk to him later when I think about it I'll ask him though pass it on to somebody else.

1355
01:32:55.951 --> 01:32:56.211
One of the younger kids . . . grandkids or something.

1356
01:32:56.211 --> 01:32:56.411
Be good.

1357
01:32:56.276 --> 01:32:56.476
Yeah that's . . . Good life.

1358
01:32:56.407 --> 01:32:56.607
Good life.

1359
01:32:56.472 --> 01:32:56.862
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y'all go to the doctor if you did need one?

1360
01:32:56.862 --> 01:32:57.062
<v PRATER>Lake Charles.

1361
01:32:56.927 --> 01:32:57.127
That'd be Lake Charles.

1362
01:32:57.057 --> 01:32:57.257
That's where they went.

1363
01:32:57.187 --> 01:32:57.387
Lake Charles.

1364
01:32:57.252 --> 01:32:57.452
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the doctor?

1365
01:32:57.415 --> 01:32:57.615
No?

1366
01:32:57.447 --> 01:32:57.647
There was no doctor in Mossville?

1367
01:32:57.642 --> 01:32:57.842
<v PRATER>I went to a few.

1368
01:32:57.805 --> 01:32:58.065
Doctor Ross was I think a colored doctor.

1369
01:32:58.065 --> 01:32:58.265
Mostly went to colored doctors.

1370
01:32:58.228 --> 01:32:58.428
Most of the time.

1371
01:32:58.358 --> 01:32:58.558
Not all the time.

1372
01:32:58.488 --> 01:32:58.715
But anyway, Doctor Ross was one doctor.

1373
01:32:58.715 --> 01:32:58.915
He was in Lake Charles.

1374
01:32:58.878 --> 01:32:59.236
That was . . . Had another doctor, but I don't remember his name.

1375
01:32:59.236 --> 01:32:59.593
But my . . . Two of my sisters had their tonsils taken out.

1376
01:32:59.593 --> 01:33:00.211
Went to a white doctor had that done. [clock chimes] Both them had theirs taken out the same time.

1377
01:33:00.211 --> 01:33:00.634
I hope the doctor hold them down and take the tonsils out. [laughs]

1378
01:33:00.634 --> 01:33:00.834
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn't numb them up?

1379
01:33:00.797 --> 01:33:00.997
<v PRATER>Yeah.

1380
01:33:00.829 --> 01:33:01.057
He kind of put them to sleep.

1381
01:33:01.057 --> 01:33:01.257
Put a little . . .

1382
01:33:01.154 --> 01:33:01.354
<v ARSENEAULT>You held your sisters down?

1383
01:33:01.317 --> 01:33:01.517
<v PRATER>Yeah.

1384
01:33:01.350 --> 01:33:01.550
They was cutting up.

1385
01:33:01.480 --> 01:33:01.680
They didn't want that.

1386
01:33:01.610 --> 01:33:01.810
<v ARSENEAULT>They were scared.

1387
01:33:01.707 --> 01:33:01.967
<v PRATER>And but he gave them a little sleeping.

1388
01:33:01.967 --> 01:33:02.167
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1389
01:33:02.000 --> 01:33:02.200
Chloroform or whatever?

1390
01:33:02.098 --> 01:33:02.298
<v PRATER>Yes.

1391
01:33:02.130 --> 01:33:02.330
Whatever it is.

1392
01:33:02.228 --> 01:33:02.428
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1393
01:33:02.260 --> 01:33:02.715
<v PRATER>But anyway he gave them a little of that, and put them to sleep.

1394
01:33:02.715 --> 01:33:03.073
I kind of helped him until he got them to sleep.

1395
01:33:03.073 --> 01:33:03.273
And took the tonsils out.

1396
01:33:03.236 --> 01:33:03.436
Got the other one.

1397
01:33:03.366 --> 01:33:03.724
I done got pretty full of it then that stuff smelling.

1398
01:33:03.724 --> 01:33:03.924
<v ARSENEAULT>Like smelling a marker.

1399
01:33:03.854 --> 01:33:04.081
<v PRATER>Then I drove them on back home.

1400
01:33:04.081 --> 01:33:04.281
Drove them back home.

1401
01:33:04.211 --> 01:33:04.439
My mom and my two sisters, yeah.

1402
01:33:04.439 --> 01:33:04.732
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they out of it the whole way home?

1403
01:33:04.732 --> 01:33:04.932
<v PRATER>Yes, kind of.

1404
01:33:04.829 --> 01:33:05.317
Kind of out of it, but anyway, they wanted ice cream after they come to.

1405
01:33:05.317 --> 01:33:05.517
Got them some ice cream.

1406
01:33:05.480 --> 01:33:05.680
Yeah.

1407
01:33:05.512 --> 01:33:05.772
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there an ice cream parlor in Mossville?

1408
01:33:05.772 --> 01:33:05.972
<v PRATER>Yes, we had one in Mossville.

1409
01:33:05.967 --> 01:33:06.325
The place that it sold ice cream and soda water and

1410
01:33:06.325 --> 01:33:06.525
<c.p>candy I believe.

1411
01:33:06.423 --> 01:33:06.623
<v ARSENEAULT>The canteen?

1412
01:33:06.488 --> 01:33:06.688
<v PRATER>Yes [ . . .?].

1413
01:33:06.520 --> 01:33:06.720
One little room.

1414
01:33:06.618 --> 01:33:06.976
Little long room and we'd go there and get ice cream.

1415
01:33:06.976 --> 01:33:07.203
I miss . . . Recall that name of that . . .

1416
01:33:07.203 --> 01:33:07.403
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it Dew Drop Inn?

1417
01:33:07.366 --> 01:33:07.566
<v PRATER>No. It was . . .

1418
01:33:07.463 --> 01:33:07.663
<v ARSENEAULT>Lula Lyons?

1419
01:33:07.528 --> 01:33:07.728
<v PRATER>Sweet Dreams.

1420
01:33:07.593 --> 01:33:07.793
<v ARSENEAULT>Sweet Dreams.

1421
01:33:07.659 --> 01:33:07.951
<v PRATER>Sweet Dream Parlor I believe they call it, yeah.

1422
01:33:07.951 --> 01:33:08.151
<v ARSENEAULT>Sweet Dreams.

1423
01:33:08.016 --> 01:33:08.216
<v PRATER>Yes, Sweet Dreams Parlor.

1424
01:33:08.146 --> 01:33:08.346
That was . . . Who had that?

1425
01:33:08.309 --> 01:33:08.509
Bradleys had that.

1426
01:33:08.407 --> 01:33:08.607
Bradleys, yes.

1427
01:33:08.472 --> 01:33:08.699
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we're at . . . What time is it?

1428
01:33:08.699 --> 01:33:08.899
<v PRATER>Yes.

1429
01:33:08.732 --> 01:33:08.959
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we've been talking for a while.

1430
01:33:08.959 --> 01:33:09.159
<v PRATER>Oh yeah well . . .

1431
01:33:09.057 --> 01:33:09.610
<v ARSENEAULT>I'll just ask you one more because I still have a lot to talk to you about.

1432
01:33:09.610 --> 01:33:09.810
Let's see.

1433
01:33:09.675 --> 01:33:09.875
What was I wanting to know?

1434
01:33:09.870 --> 01:33:10.130
Oh do you remember when y'all got water?

1435
01:33:10.130 --> 01:33:10.330
<v PRATER>[laughs] I can't remember.

1436
01:33:10.260 --> 01:33:10.813
I don't remember when that was, but that was . . . Could be in the last part of '70s.

1437
01:33:10.813 --> 01:33:11.013
I could be . . . I'm not sure.

1438
01:33:11.008 --> 01:33:11.463
But anyway, it wasn't too many houses in Mossville when they got the water.

1439
01:33:11.463 --> 01:33:12.114
But back in there where we were weren't many houses because when I built my house in '57 I believe,

1440
01:33:12.114 --> 01:33:12.407
something like that, I had a well put down.

1441
01:33:12.407 --> 01:33:12.607
I dug a well.

1442
01:33:12.537 --> 01:33:12.737
It was a [bowled?] well.

1443
01:33:12.699 --> 01:33:12.899
<v ARSENEAULT>You dug it yourself?

1444
01:33:12.829 --> 01:33:13.220
<v PRATER>No, no I had a company come to come put it in.

1445
01:33:13.220 --> 01:33:13.420
<v ARSENEAULT>Company comes and do it.

1446
01:33:13.382 --> 01:33:13.935
<v PRATER>And that well right there now when Sasol brought me out there it was 420 feet deep.

1447
01:33:13.935 --> 01:33:14.135
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1448
01:33:14.000 --> 01:33:16.299
<v PRATER>Four hundred and twenty feet deep.

1449
01:33:16.299 --> 01:33:20.513
I took the water because there's running water in here then.

1450
01:33:20.513 --> 01:33:24.344
They didn't have enough houses to really . . . To get it.

1451
01:33:24.344 --> 01:33:25.494
To put on.

1452
01:33:25.494 --> 01:33:31.623
So I went on and took the water, and I kept my well for a while.

1453
01:33:31.623 --> 01:33:35.838
I tried on that, you see, and frankly I lost that.

1454
01:33:35.838 --> 01:33:37.370
I lost it then.

1455
01:33:37.370 --> 01:33:39.286
I kept it a while.

1456
01:33:39.286 --> 01:33:41.968
Wash my car and stuff like that.

1457
01:33:41.968 --> 01:33:44.266
It was real good water too.

1458
01:33:44.266 --> 01:33:46.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it come from an aquafer?

1459
01:33:46.565 --> 01:33:48.481
Where was it coming from?

1460
01:33:48.481 --> 01:33:50.779
<v PRATER>It was coming from ground there.

1461
01:33:50.779 --> 01:33:51.545
It was . . .

1462
01:33:51.545 --> 01:33:52.695
<v ARSENEAULT>The Chicot Aquifer?

1463
01:33:52.695 --> 01:33:57.675
<v PRATER>It was down somewhere in there because I first wanted a shallow well.

1464
01:33:57.675 --> 01:33:59.208
That's a hundred-foot well.

1465
01:33:59.208 --> 01:33:59.974
Shallow well.

1466
01:33:59.974 --> 01:34:01.506
So he [bowed?] two.

1467
01:34:01.506 --> 01:34:06.104
He bowed one over here and he lost . . . The sand took it,

1468
01:34:06.104 --> 01:34:09.552
and he moved . . . I told him to move over.

1469
01:34:09.552 --> 01:34:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Does that mean it caved in on itself?

1470
01:34:13.000 --> 01:34:15.500
<v PRATER>No, no. He was small . . . Small board.

1471
01:34:15.500 --> 01:34:17.286
There wasn't about four inches.

1472
01:34:17.286 --> 01:34:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Tiny well?

1473
01:34:18.000 --> 01:34:19.071
<v PRATER>Yes, four inches.

1474
01:34:19.071 --> 01:34:20.857
It went down with it.

1475
01:34:20.857 --> 01:34:21.214
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1476
01:34:21.214 --> 01:34:24.429
<v PRATER>And the sand caught him down at the bottom.

1477
01:34:24.429 --> 01:34:25.500
See the sand . . .

1478
01:34:25.500 --> 01:34:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So he can't go any further?

1479
01:34:28.000 --> 01:34:31.310
<v PRATER>No, he could go further but the sand that's took the water.

1480
01:34:31.310 --> 01:34:32.138
In the water.

1481
01:34:32.138 --> 01:34:33.517
That's the sand . . . hit sand.

1482
01:34:33.517 --> 01:34:34.621
<v ARSENEAULT>Absorbing all the like . . .

1483
01:34:34.621 --> 01:34:35.448
<v PRATER>Quick sand like.

1484
01:34:35.448 --> 01:34:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1485
01:34:36.000 --> 01:34:36.621
<v PRATER>Sand, yes.

1486
01:34:36.621 --> 01:34:37.242
You know?

1487
01:34:37.242 --> 01:34:40.348
Could go but the sand wouldn't let the water through.

1488
01:34:40.348 --> 01:34:41.280
See the sand.

1489
01:34:41.280 --> 01:34:43.765
So I told him to move over there.

1490
01:34:43.765 --> 01:34:44.697
He moved there.

1491
01:34:44.697 --> 01:34:46.250
He had plenty of water.

1492
01:34:46.250 --> 01:34:48.424
<c.p>But the pump didn't pick it up.

1493
01:34:48.424 --> 01:34:50.288
Electric pump didn't pick it up.

1494
01:34:50.288 --> 01:34:53.705
All right, so I went . . . He stayed around a couple days.

1495
01:34:53.705 --> 01:34:54.947
So I told him.

1496
01:34:54.947 --> 01:34:58.674
I said, "Okay, move back over and put me down a well."

1497
01:34:58.674 --> 01:35:02.402
I first told him a shallow well but this time no limit.

1498
01:35:02.402 --> 01:35:04.576
I said, "Put me down a well."

1499
01:35:04.576 --> 01:35:05.818
So okay, he bowed.

1500
01:35:05.818 --> 01:35:07.682
He bowed around 300 something feet.

1501
01:35:07.682 --> 01:35:10.477
He hit water, but the sand didn't look right.

1502
01:35:10.477 --> 01:35:12.030
The dirt didn't come up.

1503
01:35:12.030 --> 01:35:15.136
It didn't look right to him so he kept going.

1504
01:35:15.136 --> 01:35:16.379
Four hundred twenty feet.

1505
01:35:16.379 --> 01:35:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1506
01:35:17.000 --> 01:35:18.471
<v PRATER>That well right there now.

1507
01:35:18.471 --> 01:35:19.647
Sasol got it there.

1508
01:35:19.647 --> 01:35:20.824
<v ARSENEAULT>They still kept it?

1509
01:35:20.824 --> 01:35:22.588
<v PRATER>I think they pulled the pipes.

1510
01:35:22.588 --> 01:35:23.765
They pulled that out.

1511
01:35:23.765 --> 01:35:26.118
I think the pipe all out of it.

1512
01:35:26.118 --> 01:35:27.294
Its 420 feet deep.

1513
01:35:27.294 --> 01:35:28.471
He put it down.

1514
01:35:28.471 --> 01:35:37.000
He told me, "Now if you want to pay the electric bill you take your hose throw it in the stich and turn it on and forget about it."

1515
01:35:37.000 --> 01:35:39.941
He said, "You ain't going to run out of water."

1516
01:35:39.941 --> 01:35:40.235
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1517
01:35:40.235 --> 01:35:42.588
<v PRATER>He told me that when he got that . . .

1518
01:35:42.588 --> 01:35:44.353
<v ARSENEAULT>It must be in that aquifer.

1519
01:35:44.353 --> 01:35:45.529
<v PRATER>That's where he told.

1520
01:35:45.529 --> 01:35:48.765
He said, "Now if you want to pay the electric bill."

1521
01:35:48.765 --> 01:35:52.000
He said, "I guarantee you won't run out of water."

1522
01:35:52.000 --> 01:35:52.275
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1523
01:35:52.275 --> 01:35:57.775
<v PRATER>You want to pay the electric bill throw it over in the ditch turn it on and forget about it.

1524
01:35:57.775 --> 01:35:58.875
<v ARSENEAULT>It won't run out.

1525
01:35:58.875 --> 01:36:00.525
<v PRATER>It won't run out of water.

1526
01:36:00.525 --> 01:36:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You just have to pay a million dollars.

1527
01:36:03.000 --> 01:36:05.570
<v PRATER>That cost me about five back then.

1528
01:36:05.570 --> 01:36:07.405
It was in the '50s.

1529
01:36:07.405 --> 01:36:11.810
So in '57 it cost me about 600 dollars to bow it.

1530
01:36:11.810 --> 01:36:16.215
<v ARSENEAULT>Did the company have a . . . There's a special company that did that?

1531
01:36:16.215 --> 01:36:20.253
<v PRATER>Yes, there was a company it was out of Hackberry [Louisiana].

1532
01:36:20.253 --> 01:36:20.987
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1533
01:36:20.987 --> 01:36:21.354
<v PRATER>Yes.

1534
01:36:21.354 --> 01:36:24.291
Company hooked it up then that was it.

1535
01:36:24.291 --> 01:36:24.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1536
01:36:24.658 --> 01:36:26.127
<v PRATER>I got a loan.

1537
01:36:26.127 --> 01:36:28.696
Had to pay that through the bank.

1538
01:36:28.696 --> 01:36:32.000
I didn't have that kind of money. [laughs]

1539
01:36:32.000 --> 01:36:35.927
<v ARSENEAULT>The bank owned that well for a little while.

1540
01:36:35.927 --> 01:36:36.364
<v PRATER>Yes.

1541
01:36:36.364 --> 01:36:37.236
Yes indeed.

1542
01:36:37.236 --> 01:36:37.673
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1543
01:36:37.673 --> 01:36:45.964
<v PRATER>That's the same way that happened . . . That property I had out there, I got a loan to get that.

1544
01:36:45.964 --> 01:36:49.891
It was 500 dollars to buy that lot there.

1545
01:36:49.891 --> 01:36:52.509
Two lots where I was at.

1546
01:36:52.509 --> 01:36:52.945
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1547
01:36:52.945 --> 01:36:54.255
<v PRATER>250 a lot.

1548
01:36:54.255 --> 01:36:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a deal.

1549
01:36:56.000 --> 01:36:56.460
<v PRATER>Yes.

1550
01:36:56.460 --> 01:36:58.302
Sasol got them now.

1551
01:36:58.302 --> 01:37:02.905
So in a way I come out good because my

1552
01:37:02.905 --> 01:37:13.032
<c.p>house was getting old and I got Sasol bought my house, so I moved over here and bought my brand new house.

1553
01:37:13.032 --> 01:37:16.254
So come out better in a way.

1554
01:37:16.254 --> 01:37:19.476
Right now I got tax to pay.

1555
01:37:19.476 --> 01:37:19.937
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1556
01:37:19.937 --> 01:37:22.698
<v PRATER>Now I got tax to pay.

1557
01:37:22.698 --> 01:37:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They always get you.

1558
01:37:25.000 --> 01:37:25.417
<v PRATER>Yes.

1559
01:37:25.417 --> 01:37:26.667
<v ARSENEAULT>They get you.

1560
01:37:26.667 --> 01:37:29.167
<v PRATER>But I don't mind it though.

1561
01:37:29.167 --> 01:37:29.583
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1562
01:37:29.583 --> 01:37:30.833
Death and taxes.

1563
01:37:30.833 --> 01:37:35.000
<v PRATER>Now I got this homestead not there, you see.

1564
01:37:35.000 --> 01:37:37.533
<v ARSENAULT>Once you work it it's yours.

1565
01:37:37.533 --> 01:37:37.956
<v PRATER>Yes.

1566
01:37:37.956 --> 01:37:40.067
That’s why I said homesteaded.

1567
01:37:40.067 --> 01:37:40.489
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1568
01:37:40.489 --> 01:37:42.600
<v PRATER>I can homestead so much.

1569
01:37:42.600 --> 01:37:47.244
They can only allow you so much for homesteading, you see.

1570
01:37:47.244 --> 01:37:51.044
So I homesteaded then I got paid the remain.

1571
01:37:51.044 --> 01:37:51.467
Yes.

1572
01:37:51.467 --> 01:37:55.689
<v ARSENEAULT>So you kept the well even when they brought the . . .?

1573
01:37:55.689 --> 01:37:56.111
<v PRATER>Yes.

1574
01:37:56.111 --> 01:37:59.489
It was there, but I wasn't using it.

1575
01:37:59.489 --> 01:38:06.667
I quit using it after for several years after they run the water I quit using it.

1576
01:38:06.667 --> 01:38:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why . . . Did they bring the water in because the water table was getting polluted?

1577
01:38:13.000 --> 01:38:13.392
<v PRATER>Yes.

1578
01:38:13.392 --> 01:38:21.614
That was a lot of it because a lot of people had little wells some of them are twenty, thirty feet.

1579
01:38:21.614 --> 01:38:22.397
<v ARSENEAULT>They're shallow.

1580
01:38:22.397 --> 01:38:23.571
<v PRATER>Yes, shallow well.

1581
01:38:23.571 --> 01:38:25.921
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was like a puddle?

1582
01:38:25.921 --> 01:38:27.878
<v PRATER>Yes, that's what I'm saying.

1583
01:38:27.878 --> 01:38:31.402
A lot of wells out there wasn't that deep.

1584
01:38:31.402 --> 01:38:32.577
Way back hit

1585
01:38:32.577 --> 01:38:39.233
<c.p>water pretty . . . Well where we stayed at though, where we first stayed at, by Old Spanish Trail.

1586
01:38:39.233 --> 01:38:41.190
That big house over there.

1587
01:38:41.190 --> 01:38:46.280
They dug a well over there after we moved. [Water when we moved?].

1588
01:38:46.280 --> 01:38:50.587
So they got together and they dug a four by four.

1589
01:38:50.587 --> 01:38:51.370
Dug well.

1590
01:38:51.370 --> 01:38:54.111
They dug that after we moved there.

1591
01:38:54.111 --> 01:38:56.852
My dad and a lot of people.

1592
01:38:56.852 --> 01:39:01.159
They just . . . Back then they'd go help each one like that.

1593
01:39:01.159 --> 01:39:02.725
Got something going on.

1594
01:39:02.725 --> 01:39:09.772
And us kids, we had to see if we pass by and see somebody out there doing something . . .

1595
01:39:09.772 --> 01:39:10.947
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all would help?

1596
01:39:10.947 --> 01:39:12.122
<v PRATER>You'd help, yes.

1597
01:39:12.122 --> 01:39:14.471
You'd go by and help them.

1598
01:39:14.471 --> 01:39:16.429
They don't do that anymore.

1599
01:39:16.429 --> 01:39:19.169
If they do they want some money.

1600
01:39:19.169 --> 01:39:21.127
But then we'd do it.

1601
01:39:21.127 --> 01:39:22.693
Think nothing about it.

1602
01:39:22.693 --> 01:39:24.651
Go on about your business.

1603
01:39:24.651 --> 01:39:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Just being a good neighbor.

1604
01:39:27.000 --> 01:39:27.491
<v PRATER>Right.

1605
01:39:27.491 --> 01:39:28.965
Right, that's it.

1606
01:39:28.965 --> 01:39:31.421
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they tell y'all?

1607
01:39:31.421 --> 01:39:36.825
How did y'all find out the water table was being polluted?

1608
01:39:36.825 --> 01:39:38.789
<v PRATER>I don't really remember.

1609
01:39:38.789 --> 01:39:42.719
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there like an announcement or a pamphlet?

1610
01:39:42.719 --> 01:39:45.175
<v PRATER>Well that's what I'm saying.

1611
01:39:45.175 --> 01:39:46.649
I don't remember.

1612
01:39:46.649 --> 01:40:03.842
I don't remember because it . . . Way back then a lot of the wells were just shallow wells and a lot of them had . . . Some of them didn't have a septic tank and stuff like that.

1613
01:40:03.842 --> 01:40:05.316
<v ARSENEAULT>The proper filtration?

1614
01:40:05.316 --> 01:40:06.789
<v PRATER>Our house there.

1615
01:40:06.789 --> 01:40:15.632
They didn't have that so now like that some of them didn't have much land and all that.

1616
01:40:15.632 --> 01:40:18.579
<v ARSENEAULT>So that's getting in the water?

1617
01:40:18.579 --> 01:40:22.018
<v PRATER>Yes, that could be contaminating a lot.

1618
01:40:22.018 --> 01:40:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Dangerous.

1619
01:40:23.000 --> 01:40:24.333
<v PRATER>Yes, and they know . . .

1620
01:40:24.333 --> 01:40:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>But yours was so deep.

1621
01:40:26.000 --> 01:40:26.333
<v PRATER>Yes.

1622
01:40:26.333 --> 01:40:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yours went . . .

1623
01:40:27.000 --> 01:40:27.667
<v PRATER>Oh yes.

1624
01:40:27.667 --> 01:40:29.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Yours went to the aquifer.

1625
01:40:29.333 --> 01:40:30.667
<v PRATER>Yes, mine was deep.

1626
01:40:30.667 --> 01:40:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Which was so clean.

1627
01:40:32.000 --> 01:40:32.333
<v PRATER>Yes.

1628
01:40:32.333 --> 01:40:36.333
Ours went deep. [phone rings] My daughter . . . My wife she'll get it.

1629
01:40:36.333 --> 01:40:38.333
Someone will get it for me.

1630
01:40:38.333 --> 01:40:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1631
01:40:39.000 --> 01:40:42.438
<v PRATER>Yes, so it was good water.

1632
01:40:42.438 --> 01:40:43.010
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1633
01:40:43.010 --> 01:40:46.448
<v PRATER>I hate . . . I really lost it.

1634
01:40:46.448 --> 01:40:49.312
You know what I mean.

1635
01:40:49.312 --> 01:40:56.188
But other than that the water . . . And the water done got bad.

1636
01:40:56.188 --> 01:40:57.333
Mossville water.

1637
01:40:57.333 --> 01:41:00.198
It just done got bad.

1638
01:41:00.198 --> 01:41:09.365
Pipes got corroded and stuff like that because they was galvanized pipes and stuff like that.

1639
01:41:09.365 --> 01:41:14.521
It wasn't a pipe that they put down now.

1640
01:41:14.521 --> 01:41:17.958
So it was thing back then.

1641
01:41:17.958 --> 01:41:21.396
Put down the wells like that.

1642
01:41:21.396 --> 01:41:27.698
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember about . . . Do you remember when the . . . Whenever the

1643
01:41:27.698 --> 01:41:34.000
<c.p>plants would like sound the alarms or anything like that?

1644
01:41:34.000 --> 01:41:45.611
<v PRATER>Yes, they put one down there around . . . They put one of them down probably where the school is now.

1645
01:41:45.611 --> 01:41:53.556
In that area I think they put one right down in there somewhere.

1646
01:41:53.556 --> 01:41:57.833
They put another one around Maplewood somewhere.

1647
01:41:57.833 --> 01:42:08.833
I think they put one like that . . . the alarm like that, but couldn't understand what it was saying.

1648
01:42:08.833 --> 01:42:17.389
When the plants would announce something like that, you couldn't understand what was said.

1649
01:42:17.389 --> 01:42:21.667
They didn't put that in until late.

1650
01:42:21.667 --> 01:42:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What would y'all have . . . Was there like an evacuation plan or . . .?

1651
01:42:29.000 --> 01:42:31.944
<v PRATER>No, they just . . . In house.

1652
01:42:31.944 --> 01:42:33.121
Stay in.

1653
01:42:33.121 --> 01:42:38.419
Put down all the windows and stuff like that.

1654
01:42:38.419 --> 01:42:41.363
Stay inside for so long.

1655
01:42:41.363 --> 01:42:45.484
They'd say when it was over with.

1656
01:42:45.484 --> 01:42:51.371
Wasn't ever nothing hardly bad that turned loose really bad.

1657
01:42:51.371 --> 01:42:56.081
You'd hear when they say what's going on.

1658
01:42:56.081 --> 01:43:05.500
Sometime you didn't know what was going on because you didn't understand what was going on.

1659
01:43:05.500 --> 01:43:15.508
You stay out there . . . Other than that a few people got sick from being out like that.

1660
01:43:15.508 --> 01:43:19.629
Other than that's another thing going on.

1661
01:43:19.629 --> 01:43:25.516
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all ever have to evacuate or anything like that?

1662
01:43:25.516 --> 01:43:27.282
<v PRATER>Not for plant.

1663
01:43:27.282 --> 01:43:28.460
We didn't.

1664
01:43:28.460 --> 01:43:32.581
I don't remember ever leaving for that.

1665
01:43:32.581 --> 01:43:37.879
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have to leave for any other reason?

1666
01:43:37.879 --> 01:43:40.234
The hurricane or anything?

1667
01:43:40.234 --> 01:43:42.000
<c.p>Hurricane Audrey?

1668
01:43:42.000 --> 01:43:45.551
<v PRATER>Well no. We went to school for Audrey.

1669
01:43:45.551 --> 01:43:48.658
Yeah, went to the school down there.

1670
01:43:48.658 --> 01:43:51.765
<v ARSENEAULT>The whole community kind of met there?

1671
01:43:51.765 --> 01:43:54.872
<v PRATER>Yes, whole Mossville just at the school.

1672
01:43:54.872 --> 01:44:04.636
I was working in Maplewood when Audrey come through, but I didn't go to work that day because it was bad weather.

1673
01:44:04.636 --> 01:44:07.299
My . . . We planned to stay home.

1674
01:44:07.299 --> 01:44:21.947
There was a little wind blowing rain and all but didn't think it was that bad because we didn't know too . . . Didn't have the weather report like you got now and all that,

1675
01:44:21.947 --> 01:44:25.497
so we didn't think it was nothing much.

1676
01:44:25.497 --> 01:44:34.818
We knowed about it, but we didn't know how bad it was and where it was going and nothing like that.

1677
01:44:34.818 --> 01:44:44.139
So thankfully my brother-in-law came by and told us everybody going to go on down to the school and all that.

1678
01:44:44.139 --> 01:44:49.465
So we got up and we went on down there and stayed.

1679
01:44:49.465 --> 01:44:56.567
We went down there and stayed until late that evening, and we went on back home.

1680
01:44:56.567 --> 01:44:57.898
That was it.

1681
01:44:57.898 --> 01:45:03.225
That was the only thing we had then up until we had . . .

1682
01:45:03.225 --> 01:45:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The rec center?

1683
01:45:05.000 --> 01:45:07.412
<v PRATER>We had [ . . .?] storms like that.

1684
01:45:07.412 --> 01:45:10.789
I said, we didn't know nothing else.

1685
01:45:10.789 --> 01:45:11.754
Had no . . .

1686
01:45:11.754 --> 01:45:12.237
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1687
01:45:12.237 --> 01:45:18.026
<v PRATER>Had nothing to worry about until Rita and all them come through.

1688
01:45:18.026 --> 01:45:22.851
So we didn't know nothing about no storms or nothing.

1689
01:45:22.851 --> 01:45:25.746
Just that one come through, Audrey.

1690
01:45:25.746 --> 01:45:26.711
And then . . .

1691
01:45:26.711 --> 01:45:29.605
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a lot of damage?

1692
01:45:29.605 --> 01:45:32.982
<v PRATER>No, it wasn't too much back then.

1693
01:45:32.982 --> 01:45:33.465
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1694
01:45:33.465 --> 01:45:35.877
<v PRATER>No, wasn't much back then.

1695
01:45:35.877 --> 01:45:41.184
But Rita come through and there was the damage then though.

1696
01:45:41.184 --> 01:45:42.632
It really did.

1697
01:45:42.632 --> 01:45:44.561
We left for that.

1698
01:45:44.561 --> 01:45:49.386
Yeah, we left all the storms that been going on.

1699
01:45:49.386 --> 01:45:51.798
We been leaving for them.

1700
01:45:51.798 --> 01:45:54.211
We go up to Arkansas.

1701
01:45:54.211 --> 01:45:56.140
Different parts of Arkansas.

1702
01:45:56.140 --> 01:45:57.105
Different places.

1703
01:45:57.105 --> 01:46:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Kind of wait it out.

1704
01:46:00.000 --> 01:46:00.512
<v PRATER>Yeah.

1705
01:46:00.512 --> 01:46:03.581
Go up there and come back.

1706
01:46:03.581 --> 01:46:05.628
<v ARSENEAULT>Better safe than sorry.

1707
01:46:05.628 --> 01:46:06.140
<v PRATER>Right.

1708
01:46:06.140 --> 01:46:07.674
Stay up there.

1709
01:46:07.674 --> 01:46:11.256
My daughter stayed in Houston for Rita.

1710
01:46:11.256 --> 01:46:15.860
We come back from Arkansas went stayed in Houston.

1711
01:46:15.860 --> 01:46:24.047
Stayed over there with her about two weeks because we didn't have any electricity in Mossville.

1712
01:46:24.047 --> 01:46:26.093
<v ARSENEAULT>The whole community lost . . .

1713
01:46:26.093 --> 01:46:27.116
<v PRATER>All out.

1714
01:46:27.116 --> 01:46:27.628
Yes.

1715
01:46:27.628 --> 01:46:30.186
All of Mossville's all out.

1716
01:46:30.186 --> 01:46:41.442
Everybody . . . Because it didn't do any damage to my house there but roof is all they had to do my house there.

1717
01:46:41.442 --> 01:46:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You lose some trees?

1718
01:46:44.000 --> 01:46:50.449
<v PRATER>No, not on my . . . I had a pecan tree, but it didn't blow it down.

1719
01:46:50.449 --> 01:46:55.607
Had a pecan tree back there and it didn't blow it down.

1720
01:46:55.607 --> 01:46:58.617
It didn't blow nothing on my property.

1721
01:46:58.617 --> 01:47:06.785
I had a peach tree over there it blowed it over but it just waved it around like that.

1722
01:47:06.785 --> 01:47:12.374
I had to put it up, tie it off, and straighten it up.

1723
01:47:12.374 --> 01:47:16.673
Keep pulling a little more slow and straighten it up.

1724
01:47:16.673 --> 01:47:21.832
But my neighbor next door he had two pine trees over there.

1725
01:47:21.832 --> 01:47:24.411
One blowed down in the yard.

1726
01:47:24.411 --> 01:47:26.991
One blowed down across his house.

1727
01:47:26.991 --> 01:47:29.140
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, pine trees . . . they go . . .

1728
01:47:29.140 --> 01:47:31.851
<c.p>PRATER It blowed down.

1729
01:47:31.851 --> 01:47:39.253
Neighbor over there it blowed across his house, and he moved out.

1730
01:47:39.253 --> 01:47:42.955
He didn't move back after Audrey.

1731
01:47:42.955 --> 01:47:45.422
He didn't move back.

1732
01:47:45.422 --> 01:47:50.357
It tore the back end off of it.

1733
01:47:50.357 --> 01:47:52.825
He didn't move back.

1734
01:47:52.825 --> 01:47:58.377
But it was dreadful looking though out that way.

1735
01:47:58.377 --> 01:48:00.227
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, I bet.

1736
01:48:00.227 --> 01:48:01.461
<v PRATER>Trees everywhere.

1737
01:48:01.461 --> 01:48:08.864
Trees . . . But they had trees across the front of where I stayed

1738
01:48:08.864 --> 01:48:09.481
<c.p>at.

1739
01:48:09.481 --> 01:48:14.416
Blowed a lot of them down in there.

1740
01:48:14.416 --> 01:48:25.519
Come back from Houston [Texas] more and more closer you get to Mossville the more damage you see.

1741
01:48:25.519 --> 01:48:26.136
Yeah.

1742
01:48:26.136 --> 01:48:32.922
We left Arkansas Sunday morning and we go over into Houston.

1743
01:48:32.922 --> 01:48:38.474
Then that Tuesday I drove from Houston to Mossville.

1744
01:48:38.474 --> 01:48:40.942
Clean out my box.

1745
01:48:40.942 --> 01:48:44.643
Ice box and stuff like that.

1746
01:48:44.643 --> 01:48:46.494
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, your fridge.

1747
01:48:46.494 --> 01:48:47.110
<v PRATER>Yes.

1748
01:48:47.110 --> 01:48:50.195
Then went back to Houston.

1749
01:48:50.195 --> 01:48:57.597
We stayed over then with my daughter about three weeks I guess.

1750
01:48:57.597 --> 01:48:59.448
Somewhere in there.

1751
01:48:59.448 --> 01:49:05.000
Three weeks before we got electricity and all.

1752
01:49:05.000 --> 01:49:05.355
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1753
01:49:05.355 --> 01:49:07.129
I know it was bad.

1754
01:49:07.129 --> 01:49:09.968
Well we are at . . . Almost at two hours.

1755
01:49:09.968 --> 01:49:12.452
Got to let you go for lunch.

1756
01:49:12.452 --> 01:49:14.581
I know we got a meeting.

1757
01:49:14.581 --> 01:49:16.000
<v PRATER>Lunch don't bother me.

1758
01:49:16.000 --> 01:49:17.065
I'll eat sometimes.

1759
01:49:17.065 --> 01:49:18.129
Sometimes I don't.

1760
01:49:18.129 --> 01:49:18.484
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1761
01:49:18.484 --> 01:49:23.452
Well if we need to come back for a follow up is that okay?

1762
01:49:23.452 --> 01:49:25.226
Maybe in a couple weeks?

1763
01:49:25.226 --> 01:49:27.000
<v PRATER>Well we kind of . . .

1764
01:49:27.000 --> 01:49:27.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4563.

1765
01:49:27.000 --> 01:49:27.200
End Session II.]
